                {choose} $\boldsymbol{\zeta}_g^{(k)}\in\partial g(\vx^{(k)})$. Suppose $\| \boldsymbol{\zeta}_f^{(k)}\| \le l_f$ for all $k\ge0$.
				Then given $\epsilon > 0$,~\Cref{alg:dad2-2} can produce an $(\epsilon,\epsilon)$-KKT point of problem~\cref{eq:model} in expectation with $O(\epsilon^{-4})$ stochastic subgradients of $f$, and $O(\epsilon^{-4})$ deterministic subgradients and function evaluations of $g$.	 
			\end{proposition}

			\begin{proof}
				Choose $\overline{\epsilon}$ and $\beta$  to satisfy the conditions in~\cref{eq:epsilon} and~\cref{eq:cond-C0-beta} and $\beta >\frac{2B_f}{B_g}$, and let $\nu$ satisfy
				$
				0<\nu \le\min\{C_0, {B}_g, \frac{\epsilon}{\beta}, \frac{\epsilon}{2}\},
				$
				where $C_0$ is given in~\cref{lem:nearfea}. Also, choose the stepsize as
				\begin{align*}
                \vspace{-2mm}
					&\alpha_k=\alpha = \min\left\{ \frac{C_0}{\beta l_g^2} ,  {\frac{1}{\beta \rho_g},} \frac{\gamma}{2l_f \rho_g}, \frac{\gamma^2}{4l_fl_g\rho_g},\,\frac{\gamma B_g}{4l_fl^2_g}, \, \frac{B_g}{4 l_f l_g},\, \frac{ \overline{\epsilon} ^2}{16\rho L_F^2},   \frac{ \overline{\epsilon}  \nu}{4 \beta l_g L_gL_F} \right\}.\vspace{-2mm}
				\end{align*}  
				Since $\boldsymbol{\zeta}_g^{(k)}\in\partial g(\vx^{(k)})$ and $u^{(k)}=g(\vx^{(k)})$, it holds $\sigma=\sigma_2=0$.	Hence, we can still obtain~\cref{eq:bd-sum-grad} from~\cref{eq:kktvio} by setting $q=1$, $S_2=N${, (i.e., taking the whole data)},  and $K=\lceil 16\overline{\epsilon}^{-2}\alpha^{-1} \Delta  \rceil$, thus a near-$\overline\epsilon$ stationary point $\vx^*$ of~\cref{eq:smoothed} in expectation can be produced {within} $K$ iterations. Now by~\Cref{thm:main}(iii) and~\cref{lem:nearfea},  $\vx^*$ is {also} an $(\epsilon,\epsilon)$-KKT point of problem~\cref{eq:model} in expectation. It takes $K=O(\epsilon^{-4})$ stochastic subgradient of $f$ and $K=O(\epsilon^{-4})$ deterministic subgradients and function evaluations of $g$.
				The proof is then completed.
			\end{proof}

