\section{Conclusions}\label{sec:conclu}
                       %\red{The last sentence is not in the right position; we should delete one sentence in this paragraph.}
We have presented a novel single-loop first-order method for solving nonconvex nonsmooth stochastic optimization with expectation constraints. Built on an exact penalty formulation that applies a smoothed penalty function on the inequality expectation constraint, our method incorporates the SPIDER-type estimation of the constraint function into the stochastic subgradient method. Through establishing the equivalence between the near-stationary solution of the penalty problem and the near-KKT solution of the original problem, we achieve an optimal $O(\epsilon^{-4})$ iteration complexity result to produce an $(\epsilon,\epsilon)$-KKT point. In terms of sample complexity, our result is lower than a few state-of-the-art results by a factor of $\epsilon^{-2}$ in either constraint sample subgradient or constraint function values, or by a factor of $\sqrt{N}$ when the constraint is the average of $N$ sample functions. Moreover, our method delivers significantly superior numerical performance over state-of-the-art methods on solving fairness-constrained problems.  

% This work introduces an efficient single-loop FOM for solving problem~\cref{eq:model}, which involves a weakly convex objective function and expectation constraints. We construct an exact penalty model by penalizing the expectation constraint \(g(\vx) \leq 0\) using a composite function of a smoothed term \(\beta h^{\nu}\) and \(g\). To solve this model, we propose a single-loop SPIDER-type stochastic subgradient algorithm.  
% Under the uniform Slater CQ for the constraint function \(g\), we prove in three different scenarios that our algorithm can find an \((\epsilon, \epsilon)\)-KKT point of problem~\cref{eq:model} in \(O(\epsilon^{-4})\) iterations.
% 						Next, we evaluate our algorithm on two datasets for two types of problems. In these problems, the objective functions are weakly convex, with the first type involving convex constraints and the second type involving weakly convex constraints. %Notably, the uniform Slater CQ holds for the constraint functions in both cases, ensuring the validity of our theoretical analysis for both problem types. Furthermore, 
% 						Our numerical experiments demonstrate that the deterministic versions of our method achieve significant performance improvements over IPP and SSG, while the stochastic versions exhibit even greater speedup, showcasing their superior efficiency.

% 						For practical problems, we adopt the parameter selections \(q\), \(S_1\), and \(S_2\) as recommended in~\Cref{cor:convexsto}. With these settings, a significant factor contributing to our numerical improvement is that the worst-case complexity between OGC, CGC and CFC for our method is \(O(\sqrt{N}\epsilon^{-4})\), which is strictly better than the \(O(N\epsilon^{-4})\) complexity of existing methods such as IPP and SSG. This provides a strong theoretical foundation for the superior performance observed in our numerical experiments.




