\section{Introduction}
Large language models (LLMs) based on transformers\cite{vaswani2017attention} have demonstrated remarkable performance in various natural language processing tasks through their enormous model size. However, LLMs, as one of the most significant computational workloads today, are challenging to deploy on edge devices due to their extensive memory and computational resources required\cite{luk2024hardware}. For instance, deploying the LLaMA-2-70B in FP16 requires at least 140 GB of memory\cite{touvron2023llama}, markedly exceeding the 80 GB capacity of an A100 GPU. Weight quantization \cite{frantar2022gptq,yao2022zeroquant,dettmers2022llm,lin2024awq,lee2024owq,li2023llm,shang2023pb,nagel2020up} is one of the most effective ways to reduce the memory consumption of LLMs while preserving performance, achieved by storing parameters in a low-precision representation.

