\section{Numerical tests}\label{section:numerical tests}
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%



 In this section we present numerical examples to illustrate the performance of the proposed adaptive \ac{dir} solver.  The realisation of this solver is conducted using the tools provided by the open source scientific software packages in the \texttt{Gridap} ecosystem \cite{badia22,verdugo22}.
 The sparse linear systems were solved using UMFPACK, as provided by Julia. We used the \texttt{GridapP4est.jl}~\cite{GridapP4est2025} Julia package in order to handle forest-of-octrees meshes (including facet integration on non-conforming interfaces as per required by the computation of the a posteriori error estimator) and \ac{fe} space constraints. This package, built upon the \texttt{p4est} meshing engine~\cite{burstedde2011p4est}, is endowed with Morton space-filling curves, and it provides high-performance and low-memory footprint algorithms to handle forest-of-octrees.
 All numerical tests were performed on a supercomputer node equipped with Intel Xeon Platinum 8274 CPU cores, with Julia 1.10.4 and IEEE double precision. We used \texttt{-O3} as the optimisation  flag for the Julia compiler. 

