We further develop from a single generator to multiple generators in order to enhance the diversity of generated candidates. 
For a single user question, there can be multiple SQL queries that correspond to fulfill the intent.
% Therefore, we expect to construct additional models with diverse ways of generation to increase the likelihood of generating correct answers through various outputs, thereby further enhancing performance. 
Therefore, we aim to develop additional models with different ways of generation to increase the likelihood of arriving at the correct answers through diverse outputs.
% To achieve this, we improve the diversity in the SQL format to develop different models.
To achieve this, we restructure the format variations with SQL to introduce greater diversity during model building.
A key aspect is the variation of SQL writing patterns, encouraging the model to attempt using more complex SQL structures, thereby enhancing its ability to cover a wider range of challenging scenarios (as illustrated by $\mathrm{SQL}_1$ on the left side of Figure \ref{fig:ft1}, which demonstrates a preference for utilizing advanced queries).
Furthermore, we also implement certain modifications to the aspect of SQL writing style, allowing the model to adopt variations in its writing conventions to increase diversity in the generated outputs (as demonstrated on the right side of Figure \ref{fig:ft1}, $\mathrm{SQL}_3$ presents a more standardized output representation).
In practice, we conduct various aspects of reformulation on a subset of challenging queries to supplement the diverse training data. 
% By leveraging these datasets, we jointly trained high-precision models with different SQL generation preferences in conjunction with multi-task data.
By leveraging these formatted and multi-task data for joint fine-tuning, we obtain high-accuracy models with diverse SQL generation preferences.

% As shown in Figure \ref{fig:pipeline}, our pipeline allows the integration of multiple fine-tuned models or even ICL-based models to generate candidate SQL queries.
