\subsection{Label Model and Syntax}
\label{sec:viaduct-label}

In the Viaduct label model, each host has a label that refers to abstract
principals.
Delegations between hosts are realized by declaring hosts with proper
\emph{abstract principals}.
For example, in the semi-honest setting where \alice and \bob trust each other
for integrity, Alice and Bob are labeled
\lstinline{$\langle$ A, A $\wedge$ B $\rangle$}
and
\lstinline{$\langle$ B, A $\wedge$ B $\rangle$},
respectively,
where \lstinline{A} and \lstinline{B} are abstract principals.
This ensures that Alice's and Bob's integrity labels act for each other
under the $\sactsfor$ relation in the lattice of abstract principals, so no
delegation context is needed.

The original Viaduct label model has three weaknesses.
First, abstract principals do not directly relate to concrete principals,
which adds difficulty in defining the attacker model.
For example, in the semi-honest setting above,
\lstinline{Alice: $\langle$ A, X $\rangle$} and
\lstinline{Bob: $\langle$ B, X $\rangle$}
is also a valid pair of labels for Alice and Bob.
It is unclear how to define a consistent attacker
model that is agnostic to the choice of abstract principals.
Second, the syntax for specifying delegation is not modular.
For example, suppose we would like to add a third host Chuck who has mutual
trust for integrity with Alice and Bob.
This would require the addition of abstract principal \lstinline{C} for
Chuck's confidentiality.
Moreover, to reflect changes in integrity, all hosts'
integrity labels must be updated to \lstinline{A $\wedge$ B $\wedge$ C}.
Finally, coming up with a correct parameterization of principals can
be painstaking when the trust assumptions are complex.

Instead of parameterizing, we designed an IFC label model that is faithful
to its algebraic nature:
principals are names of hosts,
labels are elements of the free distributive lattice generated by host names,
and delegations are explicitly specified on the side.
Our new label model addresses all three problems.
First, because all atomic principals correspond to real agents,
all labels in the model correspond to well defined security levels.
Second, adding hosts that delegate to existing hosts only requires adding
delegation statements to the delegation context.
This results in less effort in doing global reasoning about trust delegations
when specifying trust relations.
