\begin{abstract}
%Modern games rely on a vast amount of content. Trading Card Games (TCGs), in particular, are complex systems, often containing thousands of unique cards, that require carefully balanced mechanics, creative design, and continuous content updates to stay engaging. 
%Recently, Generative Artificial Intelligence (Gen-AI) has shown breakthroughs in areas such as text and image generation.
%Automating the design process with Gen-AI can accelerate development and 
%unlock new creative possibilities.

%The visual style of game elements considerably contributes to the overall experience, even beyond their actual functionality.
%It shapes the appeal to the users, while the abilities determine the actual in-game functionality.

The visual style of game elements considerably contributes to the overall experience. Aesthetics influence player appeal, while the abilities of game pieces define their in-game functionality.
In this paper, we investigate how the visual style of collectible cards influences the players' perception of the card's actual strength in the game.
Using the popular trading card game Magic: The Gathering, we conduct a single-blind survey study that examines how players perceive the strength of AI-generated cards that are shown in two contrasting visual styles: cute and harmless, or heroic and mighty. 
Our analysis reveals that some participants are influenced by a card's visual appearance when judging its in-game strength.
Overall, differences in style perception are normally distributed around a neutral center, but individual participants vary in \emph{both} directions: some generally perceive the cute style to be stronger, whereas others believe that the heroic style is better.
%In addition, we make several auxiliary observations.
%Firstly, LLM-generated cards contain several errors, such as deviations from the normal functionality of cards in the game and a mismatch of classical attributes. Secondly, we find that the strength perception for individual cards varies strongly for different participants, showing that evaluating \textit{Magic: The Gathering} is a hard task with a large room for error. Lastly, we observe a general dislike of GenAI usage in \textit{Magic: The Gathering} as voiced by participants, which should caution real-world designer's usage of GenAI in their games.

\end{abstract}

