\begin{abstract}

Multi-legged robots deployed in complex missions are susceptible to physical damage in their legs, impairing task performance and potentially compromising mission success. This letter presents a rapid, training-free damage recovery algorithm for legged robots subject to partial or complete loss of functional legs. The proposed method first stabilizes locomotion by generating a new gait sequence and subsequently optimally reconfigures leg gaits via a developed differential evolution algorithm to maximize forward progression while minimizing body rotation and lateral drift. The algorithm successfully restores locomotion in a 24-degree-of-freedom hexapod within one hour, demonstrating both high efficiency and robustness to structural damage.
%Multi-legged robots (MLRs) deployed on complex missions are prone to physical leg damage, which can alter their morphology, limiting task performance and jeopardizing mission success. However, with effective recovery strategies, these robots can restore locomotion by adapting their gait to accommodate structural changes. This paper presents a novel damage recovery algorithm for six-legged robots, designed to restore mobility after partial or complete leg loss. The proposed approach first stabilizes the robot's motion by generating a new gait sequence for the remaining legs, then optimizes each leg’s trajectory using a differential evolution (DE) algorithm. This optimization process maximizes forward motion while minimizing body rotation and lateral displacement, producing an adaptive gait that integrates seamlessly into both local and global controllers. Building on our previously developed fast and modular dynamical model for MLRs, the recovery process leverages a morphology vector to represent the MLR damaged structure. The algorithm is computationally efficient and requires no prior training, enabling the recovery of locomotion for a hexapod robot with 24 degrees of freedom (DoF) in under an hour. 

%To validate the algorithm’s effectiveness, we conducted extensive experiments across four damage scenarios — including cases with one or two missing legs — using both internal IMU data and external motion tracking for comprehensive recovery analysis. The results demonstrate the method's robustness and accuracy, highlighting its potential for real-world deployment in dynamic and unpredictable environments.

\end{abstract}



