\subsection{Damage Recovery in Multi-Legged Robots}


Damage in multi-legged robots (MLRs) can manifest in various ways, including sensor malfunctions, joint looseness or locking, and hardware failures. In this work, we specifically define "damage" as the partial breakage or complete loss of limbs, leading to morphological changes in the robot. The primary objective of damage recovery is to maintain stability while restoring locomotion.

One significant consequence of damage in MLRs is falling. To address this, Sun \textit{et al.} developed a model-free deep reinforcement learning approach for fall recovery in quadruped robots \cite{sun2024fall}. Similarly, Li \textit{et al.} proposed a deep reinforcement learning framework integrating a learning-based state estimator and a proprioceptive history policy to enable dynamic fall recovery under external disturbances \cite{li2024dynamic}. Lu \textit{et al.} introduced an adaptive fall recovery controller for quadrupedal robots navigating challenging terrains, including rocky surfaces, beams, steep slopes, and irregular stones \cite{lu2024learning}. Additionally, Ma \textit{et al.} demonstrated an arm-assisted strategy for reducing fall damage and facilitating recovery in legged manipulators \cite{ma2023learning}.

\subsection{Autonomous Recovery Strategies}

Autonomous recovery methods can be categorized into three main strategies: healing, replacing, and reconfiguring. Healing-based approaches use self-healing polymers \cite{ref20}, as demonstrated in \cite{ref21} through photo-welding on shape memory composites and in \cite{ref22} using neural cellular automata to regenerate damaged morphology. In rigid robots, fault tolerance is often achieved through replacement. For example, \cite{ref26} explores a multi-robot system where a second robot assumes the role of a failed unit, while \cite{ozkan2021self} presents a strategy where other robots assist in transporting a damaged unit to a designated location.

Among these approaches, reconfiguration is the most effective for damage recovery, as it eliminates the need for advanced self-healing materials and does not require a multi-agent system. In \cite{ref23}, a method was proposed for soft robotic systems to adapt their shape and modify their gait by drawing inspiration from various species of spiders and caterpillars. Similarly, \cite{ref25} investigated a simulated soft robot composed of elastic voxels subjected to progressive structural damage, where locomotion was restored by deforming the remaining structure without altering the pre-damage control strategy.

While reconfiguration was initially developed for soft robots, it can also be applied to rigid robots with redundant degrees of freedom (DoF) using adaptive control and path planning algorithms. Bongard introduced a novel self-diagnosing approach in 2006, in which a four-legged robot inferred its own structure by analyzing actuation-sensation relationships and then used this self-model to generate forward locomotion \cite{ref14}. Expanding on this concept, researchers in \cite{ref17, cully2015} developed an intelligent trial-and-error algorithm that enables robots to adapt to damage in under two minutes. In simulation, a high-performance behavior map is generated based on a model of the healthy robot. When damage occurs, the robot selectively tests predicted high-performance configurations and continuously refines its estimates using real-world feedback. While the Cully method achieved a movement speed of 0.2064 m/s, a more recent study introduced a constrained optimization method that further improved performance, increasing the movement speed to 0.2619 m/s \cite{ref18}.

Alternative control strategies have also been explored to enhance damage recovery. Farid \textit{et al.} proposed a fault recovery strategy for legged robots using a sliding mode controller to mitigate actuator faults such as actuation bias, effective gain degradation, and actuator saturation \cite{farid2018fractional}. Suzuki \textit{et al.} introduced a hybrid control approach, switching between a model-based and a learning-based controller to compensate for unexpected behaviors not represented in the training dataset \cite{ref27}. Feber \textit{et al.} investigated dynamic adaptation rules for learning a new gait rhythm following leg damage in a hexapod walking robot \cite{feber2022gait}. Similarly, Yang \textit{et al.} developed a robust gait generation algorithm for a four-legged robot, successfully generating a compensatory gait by simulating joint damage \cite{yang2002fault}. Johnson \textit{et al.} utilized a gait classification formalism to recover from leg failure in a hexapod equipped with a single actuator per leg \cite{johnson2010disturbance}.

Most existing works focus on modifying gait patterns to adapt to morphological changes. Therefore, it is crucial to develop a gait generation strategy that can effectively adapt to structural changes resulting from damage. Christensen \textit{et al.} introduced a fault-tolerant gait learning strategy using a stochastic optimization algorithm, which optimizes eight open parameters of a central pattern generator and converges within ten minutes, even after actuator failures \cite{christensen2014fault}. Pratihar \textit{et al.} proposed a GA-fuzzy approach to simultaneously generate optimal gait trajectories and sequences for a six-legged robot \cite{pratihar2002optimal}. Erden \textit{et al.} applied reinforcement learning to address free gait generation and adaptability in a six-legged robot, demonstrating the ability to learn a stable five-legged gait after a rear-leg failure \cite{erden2008free}. Shi \textit{et al.} introduced a reinforcement learning-based approach incorporating an evolutionary radial basis function network foot trajectory generator, enabling quadrupedal robots to adapt to diverse terrains, including slopes and stairs \cite{shi2022reinforcement}. Zhang \textit{et al.} investigated the effect of duty factor in gait planning for hexapod robots, examining tripod, quadrangular, and pentagonal gaits to minimize foot sliding and attitude fluctuation \cite{zhang2021straight}. Kon \textit{et al.} proposed a genetic algorithm-based gait generation method for damaged hexapods, though the resulting gaits were often too erratic for real-world applications \cite{kon2020gait}. Additionally, the concept of gait generation has been extended to wheeled-legged robots \cite{bjelonic2021whole}.

