\begin{abstract}
Resolving the dichotomy between the human-like yet constrained reasoning processes of Cognitive Architectures and the broad but often noisy inference behavior of Large Language Models (LLMs) remains a challenging but exciting pursuit, for enabling reliable machine reasoning capabilities in production systems. Because Cognitive Architectures are famously developed for the purpose of modeling the internal mechanisms of human cognitive decision-making at a computational level, new investigations consider the goal of informing LLMs with the knowledge necessary for replicating such processes, e.g., guided perception, memory, goal-setting, and action. Previous approaches that use LLMs for grounded decision-making struggle with complex reasoning tasks that require slower, deliberate cognition over fast and intuitive inference---reporting issues related to the lack of sufficient grounding, as in hallucination. To resolve these challenges, we introduce \ourapproach, a novel neuro-symbolic architecture that provides human-aligned and versatile decision-making by integrating the ACT-R Cognitive Architecture with LLMs. Our framework extracts and embeds knowledge of ACT-R's internal decision-making process as latent neural representations, injects this information into trainable LLM adapter layers, and fine-tunes the LLMs for downstream prediction. Our experiments on novel Design for Manufacturing tasks show both improved task performance as well as improved grounded decision-making capability of our approach, compared to LLM-only baselines that leverage chain-of-thought reasoning strategies. %We release the anonymized code and data samples from our approach as Supplementary Material.

%Here, the goal is to ground LLMs on the internal decision mechanisms of Cognitive Architectures, which, in turn, enables LLMs to inherit properties of human-like decision-making.

%To resolve these challenges, we introduce \ourapproach, a novel hybrid architecture that enhances human-aligned and explainable decision-making by integrating CAs with LLMs. \ourapproach~combines the natural language processing and generative strengths of LLMs with the human-aligned reasoning and explainability of CAs. At its core is the well-established neural-symbolic AI system, the ACT-R cognitive architecture, which encompasses perception, memory, goal-setting, and action. By using tuned ACT-R models to inform the final LLM embedding layer, \ourapproach~integrates intermediate representations from ACT-R into LLMs. This process enables LLMs to perform decision-making tasks with improved human alignment and explainability. The \ourapproach~approach is generalizable to various decision-making tasks. In this paper, we present a deployment case in manufacturing decision-making to demonstrate how this approach overcomes the limitations typically associated with off-the-shelf LLMs in manufacturing contexts, including ineffectiveness, inaccuracy, and an absence of learning. We detail the task of manufacturing decision-making, the ACT-R model used for the task, the \ourapproach~architecture, and the experiment with the evaluation.

%Keywords: LLM, Explainable AI, Human Aligned AI, ACT-R, Cognitive Architecture, Manufacturing

\end{abstract}


\section{Introduction}
Large-capacity neural foundation models, such as Large Language Models (LLMs), have gained considerable popularity for a wide range of prediction and decision-making tasks, spanning applications, such as robotics and control, neural question-answering, scene understanding, code generation, mathematical reasoning, etc. LLMs are trained on massive datasets, can be used both as discriminative scoring functions as well as generative models, and their capacity allows them to accumulate and retain vast amounts of knowledge \cite{Brown2020LanguageModels, Andreas2022LanguageModels, dong2022survey, francis2022core, hu2023toward}. 
%An LLM defines a probability distribution over word sequences, providing a probabilistic mechanism for 'generating' text. This 'one word at a time' approach, built on billions of parameters and sophisticated tokenization schemes, is adept at accumulating knowledge from numerous input-output combinations and generating human-like text \cite{Andreas2022LanguageModels}. 
On the surface, typical usage of LLMs mirrors `\textit{system-1 reasoning processes}' \cite{Sloman1996, Hagendorff2023}, from the dual-process theory of human cognition \cite{wason1974dual, kahneman2011thinking}, which provide fast, intuitive, and automatic reasoning---underpinning tasks like navigating daily environments and making quick decisions. Advancements in multi-agent LLM frameworks as well as emergent capabilities such as in-context learning \cite{CodaFornoEtAl2024, dong2022survey} have enabled LLMs to employ more sophisticated reasoning strategies, such as `chain-of-thought' reasoning (CoT) \cite{Bhattamishra2023Understanding}. These capabilities facilitate LLMs' pursuit of `\textit{system-2 processes}' \cite{TverskyKahneman1974}, which involve slower, deliberate cognition and critical thinking for complex tasks \cite{Brown2020LanguageModels, WebbHolyoakLu2022}---essential for decision-making in realistic settings. While LLMs have shown promise in this area, key concerns remain, e.g., over discrepancies between LLM inference behavior and human reasoning \cite{BinzSchulz2023,Liu2024Large}, in analyses showing that LLMs prioritize fast and intuitive ``system-1" thinking over slower and deliberate analysis \cite{Hagendorff2023}, and over issues of insufficient grounding such as hallucination \cite{chakraborty2024hallucination}. These issues raise potential concerns about deployment settings where LLMs are left to perform inference, without having been first grounded on reliable knowledge sources or decision processes \cite{yao2022react}. 

To alleviate these issues, we propose \ourapproach, which shows improved decision-making capabilities over LLMs by integrating intermediate representations extracted from a well-establish neuro-symbolic system: the ACT-R cognitive architecture \cite{anderson2009can, ACTR}. ACT-R %These representations use the well-established neural-symbolic AI system, the ACT-R cognitive architecture \cite{anderson2009can, ACTR}, which 
offers an integrated theory of the mind 
--- encompassing perception, memory, goal-setting, and action --- and has been pivotal in developing synthetic agents for learning and training \cite{anderson2019}. The representation extracted from ACT-R cognitive models serves as domain knowledge, infusing LLMs with decision-making augmentation. %\jf{Be careful about this: reviewers definitely won't like that we are implying that the knowledge from CAs acts as ground-truth, while we claim novelty in integrating that ``ground-truth" into LLMs and showing better performance over LLM-only baselines. See the conflict of interest there?}
\begin{figure*}[ht]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.9\textwidth]{imgs/intelligence_decision.jpg}
\caption{Decision augmentation using a neural-symbolic cognitive architecture approach. (1) Tasks are modeled with cognitive architecture. (2) Cognitive model used to run stochastic simulation of task at scale. (3) Synthetic data are distilled from simulation and combined with prompt requests. (4) A fine tuning pipeline is used to calibrate open source LLM to perform decision augmentation for task in exercise.
} %\jf{Text is hard to read --- it should either span both columns, or, if we do not think it is sufficiently pertinent, we can move the figure to the appendix and describe the process in text, in the main content (e.g., in a problem definition section).}}
\label{fig:taskprocess}
\end{figure*}
%
\ourapproach~uses ACT-R models to represent human repeated decision-making with learning. We infuse ACT-R model's intermediate representations with the last hidden layers of open source LLM, and add a top classification layer for fine-tuning. The architecture is then deployed in unseen decision-making tasks. The LlaMa model family \cite{Touvron2023LLaMA} was selected for this research, due to its full accessibility to network architecture, including its pre-trained weights, and its proven efficacy in prior applications involving the extraction of the last hidden layer for predicting behavior discrepancies \cite{BinzSchulz2023a}. This approach integrates the ACT-R model's representation of human-like decision-making patterns into the LLM, enhancing its ability to make decisions that are both human-aligned and explainable. The fine-tuned LLM transcends mere prediction of human decisions for unseen problems. Significantly, it outlines a road-map for enabling high-level machine reasoning through cognitive neuro-symbolic systems \cite{Oltramari2023}. \ourapproach~leverages the strengths of both LLMs and CAs by using LLMs' natural language processing and generative capabilities, complemented by the human-aligned reasoning and explainability offered by CAs.

This paper presents a deployment case of \ourapproach~in manufacturing decision-making, demonstrating how this approach addresses the typically noisy inference behavior associated with off-the-shelf LLMs in real-world settings. The task is associated with the key aspect of DFM: enhancing product development and optimizing production system performance by improving time efficiency and reducing headcount costs \cite{Ulrich1993}. In this work, we introduce Cognitive LLMs, describe our proposed framework \ourapproach, and provide  experimental comparisons on a novel task with strong baselines.

