\paragraph{Integration of Cognitive Architectures and LLMs}
CAs face limitations due to domain restrictions, which have hampered their broader application. They are incapable of processing natural language, they are limited to areas that can be described by logical rules, and they require a significant number of pre-defined rules to function. Intriguingly, LLMs \cite{Brown2020LanguageModels} offer potential solutions to mitigating these weaknesses. LLMs can process a variety of text inputs and are more flexible than rule-based systems. Additionally, they seem to learn rules implicitly, through pre-training, eliminating the need for manual rule creation. Hence, the notion of integrating CAs and LLMs is attractive, for leveraging the strengths of both approaches and thereby creating a more robust unified theory of computational models. This integration can take several forms, however, e.g., leveraging the implicit world knowledge of LLMs to replace the CAs' declarative knowledge mechanisms or to enhance their traditional symbolic mechanisms for procedural knowledge \cite{wray2024eliciting, kirk2024improving}. %In particular, these approaches include leveraging the implicit world knowledge of LLMs \cite{wray2024eliciting} and enhancing traditional symbolic approaches for procedural knowledge in CAs \cite{kirk2024improving}. 
Additional research explores how principles from cognitive architectures can guide the design of LLM-based agent frameworks \cite{sumers2023cognitive}, demonstrating a comprehensive integration effort that spans from knowledge representation to interaction with the environment. However, to our knowledge, unlike these previous efforts that incorporate LLMs into CAs, there is currently no research focusing on assimilating the advantages of CAs into LLMs. In this paper, we leverage a cognitive architecture to ground the reasoning process and outputs of LLMs; by assimilating a neural representation of ACT-R model within LLMs, we aim to enhance LLMs' human alignment and explainability. %This research, therefore, leveraging a cognitive architecture like ACT-R to provide symbolic and external structure for LLM outputs and reasoning processes. By assimilating a neural representation of ACT-R within LLMs, we aim to enhance the human alignment and explainability of LLMs' decision-making.

