\subsection{DNN-based Image steganography}

Recent advances in DNN-based \cite{szegedy2013intriguing,yi2016study,cichy2019deep} steganography have enabled efficient embedding and extraction of secret information within cover images through specialized encoder and decoder networks. Zhu et al. \cite{zhu2018hidden} introduced an end-to-end autoencoder-based image steganography method. SteganoGAN \cite{zhang2019steganogan} subsequently expanded this technology, achieving an embedding capacity of up to 6 bpp. Wei et al. \cite{wei2022generative} first proposed directly generating stego images using GANs \cite{goodfellow2014generative} without relying on separate cover images. Huang et al. \cite{huang2023steganography} optimized GAN-based steganography by introducing an adaptive GAN parameter update strategy. Tang et al. \cite{tang2024dig} decoupled adversarial attack and information hiding processes, enhancing the extensibility of the method to embed information in various multimedia carriers. Peng et al. \cite{peng2024ldstega} utilized the inverse process of steganographic diffusion models to design a comprehensive encoding strategy that integrates the generation of steganographic space and the embedding of data, effectively improving steganography robustness.
 %Despite this, FNNS methods still have limitations in robustness and minimizing image distortion.

However, these methods typically require extensive training data and considerable computational resources. Moreover, trained steganographic networks are often large in scale, necessitating covert transmission to senders or receivers who lack any steganographic tools. To simplify this process, FNNS emerged, enabling secret data embedding and extraction by adding adversarial perturbations to cover images without additional training. Ghamizi et al. \cite{ghamizi2021evasion} proposed a steganographic approach that takes advantage of a multi‑label-targeted evasion attack, encoding the secret payload as the class labels assigned to the adversarial images generated during the evasion process. Kishore et al. \cite{kishore2021fixed} proposed a method that increases embedding capacity by expanding the output dimension of the decoding network and utilizes information loss to generate encrypted images. Luo et al. \cite{luo2023securing} introduced a key-based method to synchronize steganographic tools and prevent unauthorized data recovery. Li et al. \cite{li2024cover} proposed a Cover-separable Fixed Neural Network Steganography (Cs-FNNS) method that combines secret information with adversarial perturbations and optimizes the perturbations using a steganographic perturbation search algorithm. However, FNN-based steganographic methods often exhibit poor robustness against common image attacks, which
hinders the accurate recovery of hidden information. Furthermore, these methods typically produce stego images that suffer from poor visual quality and pronounced distortion, ultimately limiting their practical applicability.


\subsection{Deep Generative Models}
In recent years, deep generative models—such as Generative Adversarial Networks (GANs) \cite{goodfellow2014generative}, Variational Autoencoders (VAEs) \cite{kingma2013auto}, Flow Models \cite{kingma2018glow}, and Diffusion Models \cite{ho2020denoising} have advanced rapidly. Training in large-scale datasets approximates complex data distributions and has been widely used in Artificial Intelligence Generated Content (AIGC), achieving impressive results in computer vision \cite{ho2022cascaded}, natural language processing \cite{brown2020language}, privacy protection \cite{yuan2022generating}, and biological sciences \cite{zeng2022deep}. For example, Rombach et al. \cite{rombach2022high} employed diffusion models to synthesize high-resolution images; Ganev et al. \cite{ganev2024graphical} integrated multiple models with differential privacy (DP) synthetic data generation techniques to mitigate privacy risks; and Lai et al. \cite{lai2025deep} applied deep generative models for drug molecule design, accelerating drug development and application. AIGC has also been utilized in information hiding. In RFNNS, by simply sharing a key and a prompt, both sender and the receiver can employ a generative model to produce identical cover images, thereby enabling precise localization of complex texture perturbations. Subsequently, on the basis of identified perturbation regions, we employ a RSPG strategy to embed the perturbations into specific areas of the generated cover image, thus producing a stego image. By integrating these techniques, our solution effectively enhances the practicality of covert communication.

