\subsection{Attack Budgets} \label{sec:budgets}

To help us understand the amount of change that is needed to be made to the abstract in order to for attacks to succeed, we explore the attack success rates under different attack budgets on $\PaperReviewerPair$ samples from the NeurIPS 2023 dataset. We study the effectiveness of each of the two abstract modification operations --- \IncludeRelatedWork{} and \InsertKeywords{} in increasing the similarity $\SimilarityAbstract{\Title}{\AbstractAdv}{\ArchiveAdv{}}$ between the adversarial abstracts and the curated archive $\ArchiveAdv{}$. We investigate the effect of the choice of hyperparameters $\Regenerations$, $\Alternations$, and $\Keywords$ representing the attack budgets used in \IncludeRelatedWork{} and \InsertKeywords{} (Section \ref{sec:author}). We report the fully automatic mode attack success rates of different values of $\Regenerations$, $\Alternations$ and $\Keywords$ on 50 samples with natural rankings of 101.  First, in Figure \ref{fig:regen}, we report the attack success rates for a range of values for $\Regenerations$ -- the number of rounds for which \IncludeRelatedWork{} is performed -- without performing the \InsertKeywords{} operation ($\Alternations=0$, $\Keywords=0$). We show that attack success rates generally increase with $\Regenerations$ in Figure \ref{fig:regen}. This is expected because language model embeddings can be sensitive to paraphrasing, so taking the most-similar attempt amongst stochastic outputs helps improve attack success rates. We also note that there are signs of diminishing returns as $\Regenerations$ increases.

Next, we report the attack success rates with different values of parameters $\Alternations$ and $\Keywords$ for the \InsertKeywords{} operation and the \FindKeywords{} routine, without performing the \IncludeRelatedWork{} operation ($\Regenerations=0$).  In Figure~\ref{fig:Sk-budgets}, we find that attack success rates increase with the total number of keywords incorporated $\Alternations \times \Keywords$. This trend can be seen through the color gradients from bottom left to top right in the subfigures of Figure \ref{fig:Sk-budgets}. In addition, for a fixed number of keywords inserted overall, we notice that inserting smaller batches of keywords over more \InsertKeywords{} iterations is helpful when compared to inserting a larger batch of keywords over less \InsertKeywords{} iterations. This can be seen by comparing the reflected squares between upper-left and bottom-right of the subfigures in Figure \ref{fig:Sk-budgets}. This result aligns with our intuition about finding keywords in smaller batches and inserting them over more iterations can improve the greedy search for a new batch of keywords by taking into account the new changes to $\AbstractAdv{}$ from inserting the previous batches of keywords. 

