	\begin{abstract}
    Connections between structural graph theory and finite model theory recently gained a lot of attention. In this setting, many interesting question remain on the properties of hereditary dependent (NIP) classes of graphs, in particular related to first-order transductions.

    Motivated by Simon's decomposition theorem of dependent types into a stable part and a distal (order-like) part,
     we conjecture that every hereditary dependent class of graphs is transduction-equivalent to a hereditary dependent class of partially ordered graphs, where the cover graph of the partial order has bounded treewidth and the unordered graph is (edge) stable.

     In this paper, we consider the first non-trivial case (classes with bounded linear cliquewidth) and prove that the conjecture holds in a strong form, where the cover graph of the partial order has bounded pathwidth. Then, we extend our study to classes that admit bounded-size bounded linear cliquewidth covers, and prove that the conjecture holds for these classes, too.
	\end{abstract}
\section{Introduction}
In recent years, there has been a growing interest in the structural study of hereditary
classes of graphs in the light of model theory. Particularly, since the identification
\cite{Adler2013} of a bridge between the graph theoretical notion of a nowhere dense class
and the model theoretical notions of stability,  dependence, and their monadic
counterparts, a particular attention has been given to (the model theoretic notions of)
stability and dependence. These studies are deeply motivated by the conjecture that
{\sf FO}-model checking is fixed parameter tractable on a hereditary class of graphs
$\mathscr C$ if and only if the class $\mathscr C$ is dependent. 

These studies recently led to structural and combinatorial characterizations of stability and dependence of hereditary classes of graphs \cite{covers,svm2024,dreier2024flipbreakability}.
Of particular interest, for example, is the characterization of  hereditary classes of graphs with dependent ordered expansions, which are exactly the classes of graphs with bounded twin-width \cite{Tww_ordered}.

Despite all these characterization theorems, the theory lacks of some description on how dependent hereditary classes of graphs can be generated from simple dependent classes, and whether they can be put in a canonical form.

In this context, the next conjecture has been proposed, which characterizes hereditary stable classes of graphs.

\begin{conjecture}[{\cite[Conjecture 6.1]{RW_SODA}}]
	\label{conj:ND}
	A hereditary class of graphs is stable if and only if it is a transduction of a nowhere dense class of graphs.
\end{conjecture}

In this line, we conjecture the following description of hereditary dependent classes of graphs.

\begin{conjecture}
	\label{conj:main}
	Every dependent hereditary class of graph is a transduction of a dependent
	hereditary class, consisting of a nowhere dense class of graphs expanded by a tree order.
\end{conjecture}

Note that Conjecture~\ref{conj:ND} implies, if true,  that Conjecture~\ref{conj:main}
holds for stable hereditary classes of graphs. Also, it is known that
Conjecture~\ref{conj:main} holds for classes with bounded twin-width \cite{SparseTww} (which have a particular model-theoretic meaning, as recalled above).

Dual to Conjecture~\ref{conj:main}, it is plausible that the graphs in a dependent hereditary class of graphs can be, in some sense, decomposed into a stable part and an ordered part.
This intuition echoes the result proved by Simon \cite{simon2019type}, which shows that dependent types decompose into  a stable part and an order-like quotient. 

Such decomposition results would rely on two notions,
 \emph{transduction pairing}, which is a strong notion of transduction equivalence
 between classes of structures, and \emph{class coupling}, which expresses that a
 structure is obtained by putting together relations from simpler structures (See formal
 definitions in \Cref{sec:trans} and \Cref{coupling}).

In this setting we conjecture the following.

\begin{conjecture}
	\label{conj:inverse}
	Every dependent hereditary class of graph has a transduction pairing with  %
	  a coupling of a hereditary stable class of graphs and a  class of partial orders having  bounded treewidth cover graphs.
\end{conjecture}

Note that posets whose cover graph have bounded treewidth are transductions of tree orders (as follows from~\cite{Colcombet2007})
and have nice properties. For instance, they have  bounded Boolean dimension \cite{Felsner2020} and bounded cliquewidth\footnote{If the cover graph has bounded treewidth, then it is an {\sf MSO}-transduction of a tree. As the partial order is an easy {\sf MSO}-transduction of the cover graph, we deduce that the poset is an {\sf MSO}-transduction of a tree, hence has bounded cliquewidth.}. %
 It follows that Conjectures~\ref{conj:ND}~and~\ref{conj:inverse} together imply Conjecture~\ref{conj:main}.

As a first step towards %
Conjecture~\ref{conj:inverse}, we study the first non-trivial case, which is the case of classes with bounded linear cliquewidth, and prove:


\begin{restatable}{theorem}{thmMain}
	\label{th:main}
	Every class of graph with bounded linear cliquewidth has a transduction pairing with a coupling  of a class of bounded pathwidth graphs and a class of partial orders with bounded pathwidth cover graphs.

	Moreover, in the structure paired with a graph $G$, the height of the partial order  is bounded by a linear function of the maximum order of a semi-induced half-graph of $G$. 
\end{restatable}






From this theorem, we deduce the following strengthening.

\begin{restatable}{theorem}{lcworders}
	\label{cor:lcw}
	A class of graphs $\mathscr C$ has bounded linear cliquewidth if and only if it has
	a transduction-pairing with a class of partial orders whose cover graphs have bounded pathwidth.
\end{restatable}

Note that if $\mathscr C$ is stable, the poset arising in~\Cref{cor:lcw} has
bounded height, and we recover %
the main result of \cite{SODA_msrw}: 
Every stable hereditary class with bounded linear cliquewidth is a transduction of a
class with bounded pathwidth, and it is actually transduction equivalent to this class.

Then, based on the notion of bounded-size decompositions (See e.g. \cite{covers}), we extend Theorem~\ref{th:main} to classes admitting bounded-size bounded linear cliquewidth decompositions.

\begin{restatable}{theorem}{thmStrong}
    \label{strong_thm}
	If a hereditary class $\mathscr C$ admits bounded-size bounded linear cliquewidth decompositions, then it is transduction-equivalent to a hereditary class $\mathscr D$ such that
\begin{itemize}
	\item $\mathscr D$ also admits bounded-size bounded linear cliquewidth decompositions,
	\item $\mathscr D$ is a coupling of a bounded expansion class and a class of posets with bounded pathwidth cover graphs.
\end{itemize}	
\end{restatable}

Note that classes admitting bounded-size bounded linear cliquewidth decompositions encompasses classes with bounded linear cliquewidth and include, for example, the class of proper circular arc graphs and all classes with bounded expansion.

The basic tool used in this paper is the notion of \emph{$(\mathscr D_1,\mathscr D_2)$-split}: the vertex set is partitioned  into a bounded number of parts, such that each part induces a graph in $\mathscr D_1$ and any two part semi-induce a bipartite graph in $\mathscr D_2$. Then, transduction equivalences are performed independently on the subgraphs induced by a single part and on the bipartite subgraphs semi-induced by two parts (see Fig~\ref{fig:split}).

\begin{figure}[ht]
	\begin{center}
		\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{split}
	\end{center}
	\caption{The transduction equivalence process as follows: each part $V_i$ is copied as $V_i^1,\dots,V_1^n$. The transduction $\mathsf T_1$ (for single part induced subgraphs) is applied on $G[V_i^i]$, while the transduction $\mathsf T_2$ (for two parts semi-induced subgraphs) is applied on $G[V_i^j,V_j^i])$. Finally, all the clones of a vertex $v\in V_i$ are joined to the one in $V_i^i$.}
	\label{fig:split}
\end{figure}

This paper is structured as follows. \Cref{sec:prelim} reviews the preliminary notions used in the rest of the paper.
In \Cref{sec:trees}, we define and study the notion of semi-plane trees on which our models for tame unstable graph
classes are built. In \Cref{sec:transductions}, we establish some transduction-theoretic tools used in the paper.
In \Cref{sec:sob}, we introduce several notions of models and prove the core transduction pairing result.
This pairing is lifted to more complex classes using decompositions and gluings in \Cref{sec:gluing}. We conclude
with some remarks in \Cref{sec:conc}.









\section{Preliminaries}\label{sec:prelim}

\subsection{Structures and logic} 
A {\em{relational signature}} $\sigma$ is a
finite set of
relation symbols with an arity.
 A \emph{$\sigma$-structure} $\mathbf M$  consists of a finite 
universe $M$ and an interpretation of  the symbols from the
signature: each $k$-ary relation symbol $U\in \sigma$ is 
interpreted as a subset $U^{\mathbf M}$ of $M^k$.  
If $\mathbf M$ is a relational structure and $X\subseteq M$,
then we define the \emph{substructure} 
$\mathbf M[X]$ of $\mathbf M$
induced by $X$ in the usual way.
We may view unary relation symbols as subsets of the domain, and write $x\in A$ for
$A(x)$.

For a signature $\sigma$, we consider the standard first-order logic over $\sigma$, which we denote by $\mathsf{FO}[\sigma]$ (or $\mathsf{FO}$ when $\sigma$ is clear from the context). For a formula $\phi\in\mathsf{FO}[\sigma]$ with $k$ free variables and a $\sigma$-structure $\mathbf M$ we define
\[
\phi(\mathbf M)=\{(v_1,\dots,v_k)\in M^k\colon \mathbf M\models \phi(v_1,\dots,v_k)\}.
\]

\subsection{Transductions} 
\label{sec:trans}
For our purpose it is sufficient
to work with the following restricted notion of transductions. 
A \emph{transduction} inputs a $\sigma$-structure 
and outputs a set of $\sigma'$-structures. 
It is defined as a composition of 
the following types of basic operations:
\begin{itemize}
	\item \textbf{Copying.} A \emph{$k$-copying} is an operation that
	given a structure~$\mathbf{M}$ outputs a disjoint union of $k$ copies of
	$\mathbf{M}$ extended with a symmetric binary relation which connects each vertex with its copies (which we call \emph{clones}).
    The atomic $k$-copying operation is noted $\interp{C}_k$.
	\item \textbf{Coloring.} A \emph{coloring} extends the 
	signature $\sigma$ by a new unary predicate 
	$U\not\in \sigma$. The output of a coloring applied 
	to a $\sigma$-structure $\mathbf M$ is the set of all 
	$\sigma\cup\{U\}$-structures whose restriction to 
	$\sigma$ is $\mathbf M$, that is, we interpret the unary
	predicate in all possible ways.  We sometimes speak of a
	\emph{monadic lift} instead of a coloring. 
	\item \textbf{Interpreting.} A \emph{(simple) interpretation} $\interp{I}$
	of $\sigma'$-structures in $\sigma$-structures is defined by 
	a formula $\rho_U\in\mathsf{FO}[\sigma]$ with $k$ free variables for each relation symbol $U\in\sigma'$ with arity $k$ and a formula $\nu(x)$ with a single free variable.
	Given a $\sigma$-structure $\mathbf{M}$, the $\sigma'$-structure
	$\interp{I}(\mathbf M)$ has universe $\nu(\mathbf M)$ and, for each $U\in\sigma'$, we define $U^{\mathsf{I}(\mathbf M)}=\rho_U(\mathbf M)$.
    The atomic interpretation expanding by a relation $U$ defined by $\rho$ is noted $\interp{Rel}^{\rho
\to U}$.
\end{itemize}

We take time for some comments about interpretations. First, it is useful to agree that if no formula is associated with a symbol $U\in\sigma\cap\sigma'$, then it is defined by defaults as itself: $\rho_U(\bar x):=U(\bar x)$.
Because of this, an interpretation $\interp{I}$ of $\sigma'$-structure in $\sigma$-structure uniquely extends as an interpretation of 
$\tau'$-structures in $\tau$-structures, for 
$\tau\supseteq\sigma$ and  $\tau'\subseteq \sigma'\cup\tau$ (where formulas defining symbols in $\sigma'\setminus\tau'$ are ignored). In particular, when defining an interpretation, it will be sufficient to consider the signature $\sigma$ and $\sigma'$ such that
\begin{itemize}
	\item no symbol in $\sigma\cap\sigma'$ is defined as itself;
	\item $\sigma$ is the set of all the relations used in some formula of the interpretation.
\end{itemize}

\emph{Transductions} are defined inductively: every atomic
transduction is a transduction, and the composition of two
transductions $\interp{I}$ and $\interp{J}$ is the transduction
$\interp{J}\circ\interp{I}$ that, given a structure $\mathbf{M}$, first applies
$\interp{I}$ to $\mathbf{M}$ and then $\interp{J}$ to every structure
in $\interp{I}(\mathbf{M})$ and outputs the
union of all resulting sets. 
As for interpretations, a transduction from $\sigma$-structures to $\sigma'$-structures
uniquely extends to a transduction from $\tau$-structures to $\tau'$-structures whenever $\tau\supseteq \sigma$ and $\tau'\subseteq \sigma'\cup\tau$. However, because of the coloring, we also allow $\tau'$ containing further unary relations, which are (by default) defined by a coloring operation. 

As a particular case, let us mention the transduction  from $\sigma$-structures to $\sigma'$-structures with $\sigma\setminus\sigma$ is a set of unary relations defined by an empty interpretation of $\emptyset$-structures in $\emptyset$-structures, which defines all the undefined unary relations by a coloring operation, then
forgets all the relations in  $\sigma\setminus\sigma'$. In the case where $\sigma'=\sigma$, we will speak about the \emph{identity transduction} and denote it by $\interp{Id}$. In the case where $\sigma'\subset\sigma$, we shall call this  transduction a \emph{reduct transduction} and the image of $\sigma$-structure $\mathbf M$ by this transduction is called the \emph{$\sigma'$-reduct} of $\mathbf M$.

It is worth mentioning that every transduction can be rewritten as the composition of the copying operation, a coloring operation, and an interpretation (in this order) \cite{SBE_TOCL}.
Of particular interest is the 
\emph{restriction transduction} $\mathsf R$, which maps a relational structure $\mathbf M$ to the set of all its induced substructures.
We say that a class $\mathscr D$ is a \emph{transduction} of a class $\mathscr C$ if $\mathscr D\subseteq\interp{T}(\mathscr C)$ for some transduction $\interp T$.

\paragraph*{Transduction pairing.}

A \emph{transduction pairing} of two classes~$\mathscr C$
and~$\mathscr D$ is a pair $(\mathsf D,\mathsf C)$ of  transductions,
such that 
\[
\forall\mathbf A\in\mathscr C\quad\exists \mathbf B\in\mathsf D(\mathbf A)\cap\mathscr D\quad \mathbf A\in\mathsf C(\mathbf B)\quad\text{and}\quad
\forall\mathbf B\in\mathscr D\quad\exists \mathbf A\in\mathsf C(\mathbf B)\cap\mathscr C\quad \mathbf B\in\mathsf D(\mathbf A).
\]


A special case if given by the existence of a bijection $f:\mathscr C\rightarrow\mathscr D$ such that 
\mbox{$\forall \mathbf A\in\mathscr C$}, $f(\mathbf A)\in\mathsf D(\mathbf A)$ and
$\forall \mathbf B\in \mathscr D, \  f^{-1}(\mathbf B)\in\mathsf C(\mathbf B)$.
Indeed, this implies that $(\mathsf D,\mathsf C)$ is a transduction pairing of $\mathscr C$ and $\mathscr D$.
\paragraph*{Class coupling.}

Let $\sigma_1$ and $\sigma_2$ be disjoint signatures, let $\mathscr C_1$ be a class of $\sigma_1$-structures, and let $\mathscr C_2$ be a class of $\sigma_2$-structures. A class $\mathscr D$ of $\sigma_1\cup\sigma_2$-structures is a \emph{coupling} of $\mathscr C_1$ and $\mathscr C_2$ if, for every $\mathbf M\in\mathscr D$, the $\sigma_1$-reduct of $\mathbf M$ belongs to $\mathscr C_1$ and the $\sigma_2$-reduct of $\mathbf M$ belongs to $\mathscr C_2$.

\subsection{Partial orders}
A \emph{strict partial order} (or \emph{poset}) is a pair $(X,<)$
where $X$ is a set and $<$ is an  irreflexive, asymmetric, and transitive binary relation. 
A pair $(a,b)$ of elements of a poset $P$ is a \emph{cover} of $P$ if $a<_P b$ and there exists no element $c$ with $a<_P c<_P b$. 
It is customary to denote the cover relation by appending a `:' to the partial order symbol 
($<:$ for $<$, or $\prec:$ for $\prec$).
Hence, $x<:y$ if $x<y$ and $x\leq z\leq y$ implies $z\in\{x,y\}$.
The \emph{cover graph}  $H(P)$ of $P$ is the graph with vertex set $P$, where two elements $a$ and $b$ are adjacent is either $(a,b)$ or $(b,a)$ is a cover of $P$. The \emph{cover graph} $P$ is the oriented graph, where $(a,b)$ is an arc if $(a,b)$ is a cover.


The \emph{tree-order}  defined by a rooted tree $(T,r)$ is the partial ordering on the vertices of $T$   with $u \prec v$  if and only if the unique path from the root to $v$ passes through $u$.
This partial order is a \emph{meet-semilattice}, meaning that every two vertices $x,y$ of $T$ have a greatest lower bound $x\curlywedge y$.


\begin{fact}
	A partial order $\prec$ is a tree-order if and only if
	it satisfies the following conditions:
	\begin{itemize}
		\item $\prec$ has a (unique) minimum;
		\item for every element $v$, the set of all the elements smaller than $v$ is a (possibly empty) chain.
	\end{itemize}
\end{fact}




\subsection{Graphs and colored graphs}  
Directed graphs can be viewed as finite structures over the 
signature consisting of a binary relation symbol~$E$, interpreted 
as the edge relation, in the usual way. If $E$ is interpreted by
a symmetric and irreflexive relation, then the structure represents
an undirected and loopless graph. When dealing with directed 
graphs we
denote edges by $(u,v)$, when dealing with undirected graphs
we denote them by $\{u,v\}$. When we speak of a graph, we
mean an undirected graph. An orientation of a graph $G$ is a directed
graph $\vec{G}$ that for every $\{u,v\}\in E(G)$ has exactly
one of $(u,v)$ or $(v,u)$ in its arc set. 
For a finite label set $\Lambda$, by a {\em{$\Lambda$-colored}}
graph we mean a graph enriched by a unary predicate~\(U_\lambda\)
for every $\lambda\in \Lambda$.

We say that $G$ is a {\em subgraph} of $H$ and write $G\subseteq H$ if $V(G)\subseteq V(H)$ and $E(G)\subseteq E(H)$.
We say that it is and {\em induced subgraph} and write $G\subseteq_i H$ or $G=H[V(G)]$ if $E(G) = E(H)\cap (V(G)\times V(G))$.
A bipartite graph $G=(A,B,E(G))$ is a {\em semi-induced subgraph} of $H$ if $E(G) = E(H)\cap(A\times B)$, and we shall write $G=H[A,B]$.
A class is called \emph{weakly sparse} (or \emph{biclique-free}) if there is an integer $t$ such that
none of its graphs contain a $K_{t,t}$ as a subgraph.
A class of graphs $\mathscr C$ is {\em degenerate} if there is an integer $d$ such that for all $H\subseteq_iG\in \mathscr C$, the minimum degree of $H$ is less than $d$.







\subsection{Width parameters}

We omit the original definitions of the classical parameters treewidth, pathwidth, cliquewidth and linear cliquewidth,
as we shall only need the following properties relating them together. These properties can also be
seen as giving alternative definitions of the parameters in terms of logic.

\begin{fact}[\cite{Gurski2000}]
	\label{fact:ws}
	\begin{itemize}
		\item A class has bounded pathwidth if and only if it is weakly sparse and has bounded linear cliquewidth;
		\item A class has bounded treewidth if and only if it is weakly sparse and has bounded  cliquewidth.
	\end{itemize}
\end{fact}

\begin{theorem}[\cite{SODA_msrw,msrw}]
	Every monadically stable class with bounded linear cliquewidth is  transduction-equivalent to a class with bounded pathwidth.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[\cite{RW_SODA}]
	Every monadically stable class with bounded cliquewidth is  transduction-equivalent to a class with bounded treewidth.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[\cite{Colcombet2007}]
	For a class of graphs $\mathscr C$, the following are equivalent:
\begin{itemize}
	\item  $\mathscr C$ has bounded linear cliquewidth;
	\item $\mathscr C$ is an {\sf MSO}-transduction of the class of paths;
	\item $\mathscr C$ is an {\sf FO}-transduction of the class of linear orders.
\end{itemize}
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[\cite{Colcombet2007}]
	For a class of graphs $\mathscr C$, the following are equivalent:
\begin{itemize}
	\item  $\mathscr C$ has bounded cliquewidth;
	\item $\mathscr C$ is an {\sf MSO}-transduction of the class of trees;
	\item $\mathscr C$ is an {\sf FO}-transduction of the class of tree-orders.
\end{itemize}
\end{theorem}














\subsection{Class properties}\label{coupling}

A class $\mathscr A$ is a \emph{coupling} of classes $\mathscr B$ and $\mathscr C$, that we can
assume have distinct signatures $\sigma_{\mathscr B}$ and $\sigma_{\mathscr C}$, if every $A\in \mathscr A$ is a
$\sigma_{\mathscr B}\cup \sigma_{\mathscr C}$-structure whose $\sigma_{\mathscr B}$-reduct is in $\mathscr B$, and
whose $\sigma_{\mathscr C}$-reduct is in $\mathscr C$.


A graph class $\mathscr C$ admits a bounded-size decomposition of parameter $p\in \mathbb N$ into a class $\mathscr D$ if there
is an integer $C_p$ such that for every $G\in \mathscr C$, there is a coloring $\gamma : V(G)\to [C_p]$ such
that for any set $I\in \binom{[C_p]}{\le p}$ of at most $p$ colors, the graph induced by vertices with colors
in $I$ is in $\mathscr D$.
We say that a graph admits bounded-size $\mathscr D$ decompositions if it admits decompositions of parameter $p$ into $\mathscr D$ for every
integer $p$ (we may simply talk about decompositions in this text as all decompositions considered will be bounded-size).
If $\mathscr D$ admits a natural parametrization into $\mathscr D_1, \mathscr D_2, \ldots$ (for instance where $\mathscr D_p$ are graphs where a certain parameter is bounded by $p$), we may
additionally require that vertices colored in $I$ induce graphs in $\mathscr D_{|I|}$.


This property is equivalent to having bounded-size bounded $\mathscr D$ covers. A class $\mathscr C$ has bounded-size bounded $\mathscr D$ covers
if for every integer $p$ there are integers $t_p, m_p$ such that for every $G\in \mathscr C$ there exists subgraphs
$G_1, \ldots, G_{m_p} \subseteq G$ such that each $G_i$ is in $\mathscr D_{t_p}$ and for every $p$ vertices
$u_1, \ldots, u_p \in V(G)$, there is $i\in [m_p]$ such that $u_1, \ldots, u_p \in V(G_i)$.

\section{Semi-plane rooted trees}\label{sec:trees}%


\begin{figure}[ht]
	\begin{center}
		\includegraphics[width=.75\textwidth]{sprt}
	\end{center}
	\caption{A semi-plane rooted tree. The larger node is the root $r$. The parent function is denoted by $\pi$. 	The ancestor relation (which is a tree-order) is denoted by $\prec$. For example, $v\prec w$.
	Green vertices are chain vertices (marked $C$), red vertices are antichain vertices (marked $A$), and gray vertices are leaves (marked $L$).
	The children of a chain vertex are ordered by $\lin$. For example, the children of $v$ are ordered by $x_1\lin x_2\lin x_3\lin x_4\lin x_5$ and the children of the root are ordered by $\pi(u)\lin v\lin z$.}
\end{figure}

A plane tree is a classical combinatorial object consisting of a tree given with its plane embedding. Equivalently, it can be seen as a tree with a left-to-right ordering of the children of each node.
We define a semi-plane tree
as a tree where internal nodes may or may not have their children linearly ordered. In this section,
we make precise their encoding as logical structures.
We define a  signature $\sigma_{1}=\{C,\curlywedge,\lin\}$, where $C$ is a  unary relation, $\curlywedge$ is a binary function, and $\lin$ is a binary relation.

The axioms of semi-plane trees, encoded as $\sigma_1$ structures are:
\begin{itemize}
    \item the axioms stating that $\curlywedge$ is the infimum function associated to a tree-order $\preceq$ (defined by $x\preceq y:= (x\curlywedge y)=x$), namely the standard axioms expressing that $\curlywedge$ defines a semilattice (idempotence, commutativity, associativity) and the special property ${(x\curlywedge y=y)}\wedge{(x\curlywedge z=z)}\rightarrow (y\curlywedge z=y)\vee (y\curlywedge z=z)$, expressing that the ancestors of an element form a chain,
    \item an axiom stating that the elements in $C$ are not maximal in the tree-order $\preceq$;
    \item an axiom stating that $\vartriangleleft$ is a partial order in which two elements are comparable if and only if they are covered by a common element that is in $C$.
\end{itemize}

It will be convenient to define additional relations and functions from those in $\sigma_1$. Towards this end,
we define the signature $\sigma=(C,\curlywedge,\vartriangleleft,<,E,L,A,r,\pi,{\rm st})$ and an interpretation $\interp{I}_{\sigma_1\rightarrow\sigma}$ of $\sigma$-structures in $\sigma_1$-structures (see \Cref{tab:sigma}).
