\lcworders*

\begin{proof}
	If $\mathscr C$ is transduction-equivalent to a class $\mathscr P$ of partial orders whose cover graphs have bounded pathwidth, then $\mathscr P$ is an {\sf MSO}-transduction of a class with bounded pathwidth, hence has bounded linear cliquewidth.

	Conversely, assume that $\mathscr C$ has bounded linear cliquewidth, by \Cref{th:main} it has a transduction-pairing with a coupling $\mathscr D$ of a class of bounded pathwidth graphs with a class of partial orders with bounded pathwidth cover graphs. 
	According to \Cref{lem:order_all}, there is a transduction pairing between $\mathscr D$ and a class $\mathscr P'$ of partial orders, whose cover graphs form a weakly sparse class.
	As the class $\mathscr H$ of the cover graphs of the partial orders in  $\mathscr P'$ is a transduction of $\mathscr P'$, (hence of $\mathscr D$, thus of $\mathscr C$), it has bounded linear cliquewidth. According to \Cref{fact:ws}, as $\mathscr H$ is a weakly sparse class with bounded linear cliquewidth, it has bounded pathwidth.
	\end{proof}


\begin{corollary}\label{lcw_dec_transeq}
If a class of graphs $\mathscr C$ has a parameter 2 decomposition into bounded linear
cliquewidth graphs, then it is transduction equivalent
to a class of ordered graphs $\mathscr D$ whose edge relation is degenerate
and whose partial order has cover graphs of bounded pathwidth.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
	This is a straightforward application of \Cref{dec_transeq} and \Cref{th:main}.
\end{proof}

Graphs captured by \Cref{lcw_dec_transeq} and not by \Cref{th:main} include for
instance proper circular arc graphs. Degeneracy is however a rather weak property in
terms of logical tameness, as 1-subdivided cliques are 2-degenerate but
not even monadically dependent.
In order to strengthen the result of \Cref{lcw_dec_transeq}, we need to consider
decompositions for every parameter.

\thmStrong*

\bigskip

We introduce the notion of a subtree generated from a set of leaves for the proof of this theorem.
\begin{definition}
Let $\mathbf T$ be a tree and $X$ a set of leaves, the generated subtree $\mathbf T\langle X\rangle$ is the
substructure induced by the set of ancestors of $X$, $\{u\preceq x : x\in X\}$.
\end{definition}

For a model $\mathbf T = \bigcup \mathbf T_i$ obtained as a union of trees and a set $X$ of leaves in every $\mathbf T_i$,
we extend the operator by letting $\mathbf T\langle X\rangle = \bigcup \mathbf T_i\langle X\cap V(\mathbf T_i)\rangle$.

