Eq.~\eqref{eq:KKT_market} is the stationarity condition of the lower-level problem \eqref{eq:MO:2}-\eqref{eq:MO:3}. Though there is no explicit coupling between the producers' objective functions, Eqs.~\eqref{eq:demand_c}, \eqref{eq:export_c}, \eqref{eq:core_c} and the slackness conditions in Eqs.~\eqref{eq:slackness_demand}, \eqref{eq:slackness_export}, \eqref{eq:slackness_core} impose couplings through bids and zones. 

We now introduce a more compact notation. First we stress that, given the dependence of the feasibility set $\mathcal{X}$ on $y$, the decision variable $x_n$ can be expressed as an implicit function in $y$, leading to $x_n \equiv x_n(y)$. This in turn, allows us to write the objective function as an explicit function of all producers' decision variables, i.e. $J_n(y_n,y_{-n})$. Then we let $w_n\defeq(\pi_{n,k}, \Delta_{n,k},x_{n,k}^z,\lambda_n^z,\mu_{n,k},\sigma_n,\delta_n) \in \mathcal{W}_n \defeq [\pi^{min}_n,\pi_n^{max}]^{K_n}\times[\tilde{\Delta}_n,\Delta_n^{max}]^{K_n}\times[0,1]^{K_n\cdot Z}\times[0,+\infty]^Z\times[0,+\infty]^{K_n}\times[0,+\infty]\times[0,+\infty]$. $\mathcal{W}_n$ is a polyhedron with dimensionality $3K_n+Z(K_n+1)+2$. The set of $l_n$ local constraints  are captured by $h_n:\omega_n\rightarrow \mathbb{R}^{l_n}$ and that of the $r$ shared constraints by $G:\Omega \rightarrow \mathbb{R}^r$, where $\Omega_n\supseteq \mathcal{W}_n$ and $\Omega \defeq \prod_{n=1}^N\Omega_n$. In particular $h_n$ and $G$ are continuously differentiable mappings. Finally, we introduce the local feasibility set for each player $n$: $\widetilde{\mathcal{W}}_n\defeq\{w_n\in \mathcal{W}_n\, | \, h_n(w_n)\leq 0\}$.

Using the previously introduced compact notation with the explicit objective function, we get $J_n(w_n,w_{-n})$.
%Notice that, given the dependence of the feasibility set $\mathcal{X}$ on $y$, the decision variable $x_n$ can be expressed as an implicit function in $y$, leading to $x_n \equiv x_n(y)$. Making explicit this dependence in our compact notation gives us $J_n(w_n,w_{-n})$.
%Considering once again the implicit dependence of $x_n$ on $y$, we can explicitly rewrite it in the objective function  $J_n(y_n,x_n(y)) \to J_n(y_n,y_{-n})$, and with out new compact form we can just write $J_n(w_n,w_{-n})$.
Thus, the $N$ optimization problems in Eqs.~\eqref{eq:single_level_problem} give rise to a generalized Nash game $\Gamma$ that we denote in strategic form $\Gamma \defeq (\mathcal{N},\prod_n \mathcal{W}_n,(J_n)_n)$:
\begin{subequations}
    \begin{alignat}{2}
       \forall n \in \mathcal{N}, \quad & \max_{w_n\in \mathcal{W}_n} && J_n(w_n,w_{-n}),\\
        & \mbox{s.t. } && h_n(w_n) \leq 0,\\
        & && G(w_n,w_{-n})\leq 0.
    \end{alignat}
    \label{eq:GNG}
\end{subequations}

\section{Theoretical Framework}
\label{sec:analysis}
%We present now two way to interpret the problem theoretically: first, we maintain each player separated, creating a Generalized Nash Game (GNG), then we show that we can obtain a Generalized Potential Game (GPG) if we consider all the players together. 
In a large part of the literature, the convexity of the agents' optimization problems is an essential assumption under which noncooperative games are studied and analyzed. On the contrary, in this paper, $\Gamma$ is a generalized Nash game with side constraints, involving trilinear objective functions and nonconvex constraints thus giving rise to a nonconvex game. In order to proceed we will rely on results established in \cite{pang_nonconvex_2011}, where the authors study a class of problems with similar settings. To that purpose, we first introduce a penalized version of our problem, which is more tractable for this analysis, providing results regarding the equivalence with the non-penalized version; then we present the settings of \cite{pang_nonconvex_2011}, which we use to prove existence of market equilibrium under mild assumptions. Finally we highlight the potential structure of $\Gamma$.

\subsection{Penalized Generalized Nash Game}
\label{sec:penal_gng}
We consider a penalized version of our problem, which makes it more tractable and it will allow us to use mild assumptions to obtain an equilibrium existence guarantee.
We add the constraints in Eqs.~\eqref{eq:demand_c} and \eqref{eq:core_c} to the objective function, which gives the function $J_n^p(w)$ and the new constraint maps $h_n^p(w_n)$ and $G^p(w)$ (see Appendix~\ref{app:penal_obj} for more details).
We can formulate the penalized generalized Nash game $\Gamma^p$ as follows:
\begin{subequations}
    \begin{alignat}{2}
        \forall n \in \mathcal{N}, \quad & \min_{w_n\in \mathcal{W}_n} && -J_n^p(w)\\
        & \mbox{s.t. } && h_n^p(w_n) \leq 0\\
        & && G^p(w_n,w_{-n})\leq 0
    \end{alignat}
    \label{eq:penalized_GNG}
\end{subequations}

\begin{proposition}
\label{prop:equivalence}
    If $K_n\geq \frac{Z}{3}$, there exists a set of penalties such that a solution of the penalized game \eqref{eq:penalized_GNG} is a solution of $\Gamma$.
\end{proposition}
Proposition~\ref{prop:equivalence} results from the comparisons of the sets of KKTs of the generalized Nash game $\Gamma$ and the penalized game \eqref{eq:penalized_GNG} denoted $\Gamma^p$. Details can be found in Appendix~\ref{app:proof_equivalence}.

Thanks to this reformulation we obtain copositive Hessians matrices associated to the constraints $h_n^p(w_n)$ and $G^p(w)$, which is the key point in obtaining an existence result. We discuss the form of these matrices in Appendix~\ref{app:hessians}.

\subsection{Nonconvex Game with Side Constraints}
The formulation of \cite{pang_nonconvex_2011} considers a problem where the coupling constraints are treated as penalties with associated prices, in a modified objective function. This problem is then solved as an $(N+1)$-player game, where $N$ players optimize their variables and an external one optimizes the prices, which are shared by all players. 

With the compact notation previously introduced, we can write the problem in Eq.~\eqref{eq:penalized_GNG} as an $(N+1)$-player game with prices\footnote{Notice that to directly extend the results of \cite{pang_nonconvex_2011}, we now work with the negative of the objective function and in minimization settings.}:
\begin{equation}
    \forall n \in \mathcal{N}, \quad \min_{w_n\in \widetilde{\mathcal{W}}^p_n}\left[ -J_n^p(w_n,w_{-n}) + \sum_{j=1}^r\rho_jG_j^p(w) \right]
    \label{eq:priced_game}
\end{equation}
where we call the externally optimized price, $\rho\in\mathbb{R}^r_+$ and introduce the penalized local feasibility set $\widetilde{\mathcal{W}}_n^p\defeq\{w_n\in \mathcal{W}_n\, | \, h_n^p(w_n)\leq 0\}$. We can now recall the definition of Nash Equilibrium \cite[Definition 1]{pang_nonconvex_2011} that is a relevant solution concept to analyze this class of games:
\begin{definition}[Nash Equilibrium with Side Constraints]
\label{def:NE_priced}
    A Nash equilibrium (NE) for the game \eqref{eq:priced_game} is a strategy-price tuple $(w^\star,\rho^\star)$, such that for all $n$:
    \begin{equation*}
        w_n^\star\in\argmin_{w_n\in \widetilde{\mathcal{W}}_n}\left[-J_n(w_n,w_{-n}^\star) + \sum_{j=1}^r\rho_j^\star G_j(w_n,w_{-n}^\star)\right],
    \end{equation*}
    and
    \begin{equation*}
        0\leq\rho^\star \perp G(w^\star)\leq 0.
    \end{equation*}
\end{definition}




\subsection{Existence of a Generalized Nash Equilibrium for $\Gamma$}
\label{sec:existence_NE}
To obtain existence result for the penlaized problem, we have to verify assumptions on the variables' domain, the objective functions and the constraint sets. Thanks to our penalized reformulation, we only need to verify a set of mild assumptions:
\begin{assumptions}%[{\cite[Assumptions A',B,C']{pang_nonconvex_2011}}]
\label{ass:existence}
We assume the following:
    \begin{itemize}
        \item[(a)] $\mathcal{W}_n$ are polyhedral and compact for all $n$;
        \item[(b)] $J_n(\cdot,w_{-n})$ is continuously differentiable in $w_n$, $h_n(\cdot)$ and $G(\cdot,w_{-n})$ are twice continuously differentiable in $w_n$ for all $n$;
        \item[(c)] There exists $w^\sharp \in \mathcal{W}:=\prod_{n\in\mathcal{N}}\mathcal{W}_n$ such that $h_n(w_n^\sharp)<0,\,\forall n \in \mathcal{N}$ and $G(w^\sharp)<0$;
        %\item[(d)] The Hessians matrices $\nabla^2 h_n^p(w_n)$ are copositive; %on the tangent cone $\mathcal{T}(\mathcal{W}_n,w_n^\sharp)$.
        %\item[(e)] The Hessians matrices $\nabla^2 G^p(w)$ are copositive. %on the tangent cone $\mathcal{T}(\Xi,w^\sharp)$. %In particular in \cite{pang_nonconvex_2011} the co-positivity is required on specific tangent cones, but due to our penlaized formulation we obtain constant Hessians, which respect the assumptions without needing to be evaluated on specific points.
        %\item the set $\{w \in \Xi \, | \, (w-w^\sharp)^\top \mathrm{col}((\nabla_{w_n}J_n(w_n,w_{-n}))_n) > 0\}$ is bounded (possibly empty).
    \end{itemize}
\end{assumptions}
\begin{theorem}[Existence of a solution for $\Gamma$]
\label{th:existence}
    Define $\mathcal{W}^\star\subseteq \mathcal{W}$ such that for any $w\in\mathcal{W}^\star$, the Hessian matrix of the objective function is positive semi-definite. Then, there exists a generalized Nash equilibrium on $\mathcal{W}^\star$ solution of $\Gamma^p$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Assumption~\ref{ass:existence} (a) holds, since $\mathcal{W}_n$ is defined as a polyhedral and compact set, i.e., we can express any condition of the form $x\in[x_a,x_b]$ as a set of inequalities $x\leq x_b,x\geq x_a$, which are defined as intersections between closed half spaces. Further, the objective function is continuous and differentiable everywhere in the domain $\mathcal{W}^\star$, and the constraints functions are  bilinear or trilinear. Thus, they are twice continuous and differentiable over the whole domain $\mathcal{W}^\star$. Assumption~\ref{ass:existence} (b) is then satisfied. Assumption~\ref{ass:existence} (c) requires the existence of an interior point. Considering $w^\sharp$ that strictly satisfies Eq.~\eqref{eq:demand_c}, \eqref{eq:accepted_c}, \eqref{eq:export_c} and \eqref{eq:core_c}, we immediately obtain that $\lambda_n^z = 0, \mu_{n,k} = 0, \sigma_n = 0, \delta_n = 0,\,\forall k\in[1,K_n],\,\forall n\in\mathcal{N},\,\forall z\in\mathcal{Z}$. Eq.~\eqref{eq:KKT_market} needs instead to be relaxed to a strict inequality.
%Finally, to check for Assumptions~\ref{ass:existence} (d)-(e), we need to compute the Hessians matrices associated to the constraints functions. Regarding the local set of constraints $h_n^p(w_n)$, we obtain $\nabla^2 h_n^p(w_n) = 0,\, \forall n\in\mathcal{N},\,\forall w_n\in\mathcal{W}_n$, except for Eq.~\eqref{eq:KKT_market}, for which the Hessian (we leave the full form in Appendix~\ref{app:hessians}) is easily obtained and it satisfies the copositivity requirement. Considering the only remaining global constraint, Eq~\eqref{eq:export_c}, we obtain a constant positive Hessian (full computation in the Appendix), which directly satisfies the copositivity assumption.
We then apply \cite[Theorem 13]{pang_nonconvex_2011} to the $(N+1)-$players game to obtain the existence of a generalized Nash equilibrium for $\Gamma^p$. 
\end{proof}
\begin{corollary}
  The generalized Nash game $\Gamma$ defined in (13) admits a solution.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
    Using results from \cite[Sec. 2.1]{pang_nonconvex_2011}, we have an equivalence between the Nash equilibrium of the $(N+1)-$players game and the associated generalized Nash game, which in our case is the penalized version $\Gamma^p$. We conclude using Proposition~\ref{prop:equivalence}, which states the equivalence between $\Gamma^p$ and $\Gamma$.
\end{proof}

We conclude this section checking the potential structure of the game:
\begin{proposition}
\label{prop:GPG}
    The generalized Nash game described in Eq.~\eqref{eq:GNG} is a generalized potential game (GPG).
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
    Using \cite[Def. 2.1]{facchinei_decomposition_2011},  two requirements need to be satisfied for a GNG to be a GPG. The first requirement amounts to have a feasible set that is nonempty, which is easily satisfied in our setting. In particular, the feasible set, which we call $C$, does not have to be the cartesian product of the players' feasible sets, meaning that $C\subseteq \prod_{n=1}^N \bar{\mathcal{W}}_n(w_{-n})$, where $\bar{\mathcal{W}}_n(w_{-n})\defeq\{w_n\in \mathcal{W}_n\, | \, h_n(w_n)\leq 0, G(w)\leq0\}$.
    The second requirement is for a potential $P(w)$ function to exist, which in our case is satisfied by using $P(w):=\sum_{n=1}^N J_n(w)$.
\end{proof}
From Proposition~\ref{prop:GPG}, we can rewrite Eqs.~\eqref{eq:GNG} as:
\begin{subequations}
    \label{eq:GPG}
    \begin{align}
    \max_{w\in W} & \quad \sum_{m=1}^N J_n(w_m,w_{-m}),\\
    \text{s.t.} & \quad h_n(w_n) \leq 0, \forall n,\\
    & \quad G(w_n,w_{-n})\leq 0,\,\forall n,
\end{align}
\end{subequations}
which can be solved using a standard nonlinear programming solver. %The potential structure is also interesting algorithmically, as it can be used to obtain a convergence guarantee for the best-response algorithm.

\section{Algorithmic Solutions}
\label{sec:algorithms}
We solve the market problem relying on integrated optimization and Gauss-Seidel best-response method, that we compare against a MARL algorithm. For the integrated optimization approach we use the MINOS solver, which relies on a projected Lagrangian method \cite{noauthor_minos_nodate}. Next, we discuss the best-response algorithm we use and how we built the multi-agent simulation.

\subsection{Gauss-Seidel Best Response}
\label{sec:gsbr}
We follow the implementation of the Gauss-Seidel Best Response algorithm from \cite{facchinei_decomposition_2011}.
Here we remove the coordination between players, and we solve the problem in Eq.~\eqref{eq:GNG} for each agent iteratively (once again with the MINOS solver), stopping the whole process once the decision variables of the agents do not change anymore, as summarized in Alg.~\ref{alg:gauss_seidel}.
\begin{algorithm}[tb]
\caption{Gauss-Seidel Best Response.}
\label{alg:gauss_seidel}
\begin{algorithmic}[1] %[1] enables line numbers
\State Initialize problem with feasible point $w^0=[w_1^0,\dots,w_N^0]$, $k=0$
\While{Termination criterion is false}
\For{$n\in[1,\dots,N]$}
\State Solve regularized Eq.~\eqref{eq:GNG} for agent $n$ with $[w_1^{k+1},\dots,\allowbreak w_n^k,\dots,\allowbreak w_N^{k+1}]$
\EndFor
\State Update regularization parameter
\State $k\leftarrow k+1$
\EndWhile
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}
The integrated solution can be seen as a coordinated solution for all the producers at once, while using the Best-Response algorithm allows for each producer to optimize its strategy alone. In the results section we will explain more in details how these two solutions differ in modeling the interactions between agents.

Furthermore, from \cite[Theorem 5.2]{facchinei_decomposition_2011} and the potential structure of our game, we have a guarantee of convergence to a Nash equilibrium, provided it exists, which we already proved in Section~\ref{sec:existence_NE}.

These two approaches allow us to compare fully coordinated and uncoordinated interaction schemes between agents, that we will compare against the multi-agent simulation based on reinforcement learning.

\subsection{Multi Agent Deep Reinforcement Learning}
\label{sec:marl}
The core idea of reinforcement learning is to have an agent learning directly from exposure to an unknown environment. It is formally modeled as a Markov Decision Process (MDP) $\{S,A,r\}$ where $S$ is the state space, $A$ the action space and $r:S\times A\to\mathbb{R}$ the reward function. We define the discounted return over a trajectory of length $L$ as $R(s,a) := \sum_{t=0}^{t=L-1}\gamma^tr(s_t,a_t)$, where $0 < \gamma < 1$ is the discount factor. An agent interacts with the environment sampling action from its policy $a\sim\mu(s)$, and it aims at maximizing the reward, so the goal is to find the policy that maximizes the discounted return over an infinite horizon. In particular, defining the $Q$-function as:
\begin{equation*}
    Q_{\mu}(s,a) = \mathbb{E}[R(s,a)|\mu,s_0=s,a_0=a],
\end{equation*}
we want to solve the following optimization to obtain the optimal policy $\pi^*$:
\begin{equation*}
    Q^*(s,a) = Q_{\mu^*}(s,a) = \max_{\mu} Q_{\mu}(s,a).
\end{equation*}
Generally, we do not have access to analytical form for neither $Q$ nor $\mu$, making the optimization extremely complex to solve. RL algorithms, and in particular \textit{deep} RL (DRL) algorithms, try to solve this problem learning a policy that approximates the optimal one. DRL employs neural networks to model both the policy $\mu$ and the $Q$-function, so that the resulting algorithm is model free and can be used for continuous or discrete states and actions. We will not explore in depth here the differences between DRL algorithms, but we will provide a quick overview of the one we use.

We implemented the multi-agent deep reinforcement learning algorithm described in \cite{lowe_multi-agent_2017}: this is called an \textit{actor-critic} algorithm, where an actor learns the policy and a critic the $Q$-function. The actor and critic are neural networks parameterized by $\theta$ and $\omega$ respectively, which are trained over specific losses. In particular the loss function for the critic is based on the Bellman's equation:
\begin{equation*}
    \mathcal{L}_{Q} = \mathbb{E}_{(s,a,r,s')\sim\mathcal{D}}\left[ (Q_{\omega}(s,a)-(r+\beta Q_{\omega}(s',\mu_{\theta}(s'))))^2 \right],
\end{equation*}
where $\mathcal{D}$ is a buffer where we save previously encountered states, the actions that the agent used and the reward it obtained.
The actor instead minimizes the following loss:
\begin{equation*}
    \mathcal{L}_{\mu} = -\mathbb{E}_{s\sim\mathcal{D}}\left[Q_{\omega}(s,\mu_{\theta}(s))\right]
\end{equation*}
The intuition behind this algorithm is that the critic learns to map future rewards to specific states and actions (which is the $Q$-function), while the actor tries to maximize the $Q$-function itself (represented by the critic output). In the multi-agent settings we appply a modified version of the single agent implementation presented here, where each agent has its own actor and critic, but the critics receive as input the actions \textit{of all the agents}. This was introduced in \cite{lowe_multi-agent_2017} as a way of stabilizing the learning and improving the results. In particular, given the zonal nature of our environment, we share the actions between the agents of the same zone, but not between all agents. In Alg.~\ref{alg:zMADDPG} we report in pseudo-code the structure for the training of the RL agents.

We leave further details on the implementation and references to the code in Appendix~\ref{app:maddpg}.
\begin{algorithm}[h]
\caption{Basic structure of the zonal MARL algorithm.}
\label{alg:zMADDPG}
\begin{algorithmic}[1] %[1] enables line numbers
\State Initialize problem state $s_0$
\For{$t\in L$}
\State Get action $a_n^t\leftarrow\mu_n(s_t), \forall n \in \mathcal{N}$ 
\State Clear market and obtain $(r^t_n,s_{t+1})\;\forall n\in\mathcal{N}$
\State Build zonal action: $a(z)^t=[a_n^t\;\forall n\in\mathcal{N}(z)]$
\State Store $(s_t,a(z)^t,r^t_n,s^{t+1})$ for each agent
\For{$z\in\mathcal{Z}$}
\For{$n\in\mathcal{N}(z)$}
\State Sample $(s,a(z),r_n,s')$ from $\mathcal{D}$
\State Train critic $Q_n$ with $\mathcal{L}_Q(s,a(z),r_n,s')$
\State Train actor $\mu_n$ with $\mathcal{L}_{\mu}(s,Q_n)$
\EndFor
\EndFor
\EndFor
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

