We start by analyzing the convergence and stability of the multi-agent simulation. Our settings are particularly complicated, since the market environment is dependent on all agents decisions. We tested two settings for the MARL algorithm: one with 4 learning based producers, and one with 8. In the first case, the 4 other producers always bid their marginal price. The second case creates a very dynamic environment, since at every step the price of the market is decided based on the actions of 8 independent RL agents. To assess the stability and convergence, we consider the reward of each agent throughout the training. More precisely, we compute the average reward during an episode, this being a full pass through the data. With this data we obtain Fig.~\ref{fig:8MARL_reward}, where we plot the episodic average reward for each agent divided by zone, considering the case with only RL based producers. Notice that the reward is not the profit, as we mention in the full description of the algorithm in the Appendix. From Fig.~\ref{fig:8MARL_reward} we can see that each agent increases its reward during the initial episodes and eventually plateaus to a stable value. These results are particularly interesting, since they show how the algorithm is well suited for a medium scale simulation with competitive learning agents, providing results that we can compare with the classical solutions.

We want to stress here that, using the MARL algorithm, we compute a stationary solution for the market, without a guarantee of it being an equilibrium.

\subsection{MARL Solution and Classical Solutions}
We first discuss the theoretical difference between the integrated solution and the Best-Response solution. Starting from the potential problem, we consider the role of the stationarity condition coming from Eq.~\eqref{eq:lagrangian}, which for player $n$, bid $k$ and zone $z$ is the following:
\begin{equation}
\begin{split}
    &\nabla_{x_{n,k}^z}L(x;\lambda,\mu,\sigma,\delta) = \Delta_{n,k}\pi_{n,k}-\lambda_z\Delta_{n,k}+\mu_{n,k}+\\
    & + (1-\mathbb{I}_{z = z(n)})\sigma_{z(n)}\Delta_{n,k} -\mathbb{I}_{z = z(n)}\delta_{z(n)}\Delta_{n,k}.
    \label{eq:stationarity_condition}
\end{split}
\end{equation}
Notice that every $\mu$ must be always $0$, since every other term is multiplied by $\Delta$ and can be grouped. Furthermore, $\delta$ will be $0$ for most realistic cases, since the core portion is usually a small fraction of the total demand, meaning that the constraint is always saturated. $\sigma$ on the other hand may be different from $0$, which means that $\lambda$ must be different from $0$ too, given the signs in the equation. Lastly, we highlight that $\pi$ is indexed by agent and bid, while $\lambda$ by zone, which means that, in most cases, the prices have to be equal to $\lambda$, virtually creating a pay-as-clear market.
On the other hand, when using the Best Response algorithm, every agent computes its own $\lambda$, meaning that they are not forced to bid at the same price, and they can actually bid at very high prices with no repercussions.
This constraint creates two very different markets, providing two different solutions to analyze.

Regarding the solution obtained from the MARL algorithm, even if there are  less theoretical guarantees, we can consider it as a more realistic simulation of the market, since every producer has access only to its own information and its only aim is to increase its profit.

We start by analyzing the average cost to run the market. This value is computed by summing the average profits of each producer over the full data available. In particular, for the MARL producers, we consider the cost for the final episode of their training, where we assume they reached stable bidding strategies. The results are reported in Fig.~\ref{fig:avg_cost}. We can immediately see how the two exact algorithms produce higher costs than the learning-based counterpart, which can be explained by the form of the constraints, as detailed above, and by the lack of actual competition. In the integrated solution, the problem is solved by an exogenous external coordinator, so by definition there will be no competition; while for the best response, each producer is free to update its decision variables uncoupled from the others. We can see the average cost for the MARL simulation with only 4 learning producers is very low, but this is also affected by the presence of static producers that always bid at marginal cost, driving the electricity price down.
\begin{figure}[h]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=0.9\linewidth]{plots/double_avg_cost.pdf}
    \caption{Average cost of running the market, obtained with different algorithms.}
    \label{fig:avg_cost}
\end{figure}

It is interesting to analyze how the profit is shared between producers in the different solutions. We do so by computing the Gini index \cite{gini_measure_1936}, which, given a set of profits $p_1\leq p_2\leq\dots\leq p_n$, can be obtained as:
\begin{equation*}
    g = \frac{1}{n}\left(n+1-2\left(\frac{\sum_{i=1}^n(n+1-i)p_i}{\sum_{i=1}^np_i}\right)\right).
\end{equation*}
The lower $g$ is, the more equal is the sharing of profits. We report the results of this analysis in Tab.~\ref{tab:gini}. We can see that, even if the BR and potential solutions produce higher costs, they also obtain a better overall equality index, with the two MARL based simulations getting the highest index. Once again this can be explained by the fact that the multi-agent simulation is unregulated: each learning agent can bid anything it wants, which creates a situation where few producers can drive others' profit almost to zero, using their market power. Looking at the index computed by zone, the differences between classical and learning-based algorithm stay the same.
