\section{KKT Reformulation Details}
\label{app:kkt}
The full form of the Lagrangian for the market clearing problem of Eq.~\eqref{eq:market_clearing} is the following:
\begin{equation}
    \begin{split}
        L(x;&\lambda,\mu,\sigma,\delta) = \sum_{n\in\mathcal{N}}\sum_{k=1}^{K_n} \left( \sum_{z\in\mathcal{Z}} x_{n,k}^z \right) \Delta_{n,k} \pi_{n,k}\\
        +& \sum_{z\in\mathcal{Z}} \lambda_z \left( D_z - \sum_{n\in\mathcal{N}}\sum_{k=1}^{K_n} x_{n,k}^z\Delta_{n,k} \right)\\
        +& \sum_{n\in\mathcal{N}}\sum_{k=1}^{K_n} \mu_{n,k} \left(\sum_{z\in\mathcal{Z}}x_{n,k}^z - 1 \right)\\
        +& \sum_{z\in\mathcal{Z}}\sigma_z\left(\sum_{n\in \mathcal{N}(z)} \sum_{k=1}^{K_n} \left(\sum_{z'\neq z} x_{n,k}^{z'}\right)\Delta_{n,k} - E_z \right)\\
        +&\sum_{z\in\mathcal{Z}}\delta_z\left(C_z - \sum_{n\in \mathcal{N}(z)}x_{n,k}^z\Delta_{n,k}\right),
    \end{split}
\end{equation}
where we explicit the dual variables $\lambda\in\mathbb{R}_{\geq0}^Z, \mu\in\mathbb{R}_{\geq0}^B, \sigma\in\mathbb{R}_{\geq0}^Z, \delta\in\mathbb{R}_{\geq0}^Z$.



\section{Penalized Objective Function}
\label{app:penal_obj}
We report here the explicit form for the penalized objective function used in the penalized GNG of Eqs.~\eqref{eq:penalized_GNG}. As described in Section~\ref{sec:penal_gng}, we relax constraints Eqs.~\eqref{eq:demand_c}-\eqref{eq:core_c}, to obtain:
\begin{subequations}
    \begin{align}
    J_n^p(w) & = \sum_{1\leq k\leq K}\sum_{z\in\mathcal{Z}}\pi_{n,k}\Delta_{n,k}x_{n,k}^z \\
    & + \sum_{z\in\mathcal{Z}}M_{D_z}\left( \sum_{n\in\mathcal{N}}\sum_{1\leq k\leq K} x_{n,k}^z\Delta_{n,k}-D_z\right)\\
    & + M_C\left( \sum_{j\in z(n)}\sum_{0\leq k\leq K}x_{j,k}^{z(n)}\Delta_{j,k}-C_{z(n)}\right).
\end{align}
\label{eq:penalized_objective_function}
\end{subequations}

\section{Proof of Proposition~\ref{prop:equivalence}}
\label{app:proof_equivalence}
We call the two Lagrangian functions associated to the problems $\mathcal{L}_n^P$ and $\mathcal{L}_n^U$, and we consider the solution for $U$: $(w_n^*,\Theta_n^*)\in\mbox{SOL}\{\mbox{KKT of }U\}$, where $\Theta$ is the set of Lagrangian multipliers. This solution must respect all the constraints and be a solution of $\nabla\mathcal{L}^U(w^*_n,\Theta^*_n)=0$. We now call the part of Lagrangian multipliers that are still present in $P$ $\Theta[\Sigma_n]$, and we consider the set of equations coming from $\nabla\mathcal{L}^P(w_n,\Theta_n[\Sigma_n]; M_n)=0$, where $M_n$ is the set of penalties for agent $n$. If we evaluate these equations in $(w_n^*, \Theta_n^*[\Sigma_n])$, we can solve them w.r.t. the penalties $M_n$, obtaining $\Bar{M}_n$. Given the assumptions, solving the system $\nabla\mathcal{L}^P(w_n,\Theta_n[\Sigma];\Bar{M}_n)=0$ in $(w_n,\Theta_n[\Sigma])$ provides $\tilde{w}_n$ which is a solution also for $\nabla\mathcal{L}^U(w_n,\Theta_n)=0$.

\section{Hessians Analysis}
\label{app:hessians}
For the stationarity condition of Eq.~\eqref{eq:KKT_market} we obtain the following cases:
\begin{equation*}
x_n^{\top}\nabla_{x_n}^2\left(\nabla_{x_{n,k}^z} L\right)x_n = 
    \begin{cases}
        \pi_{n,k}-\lambda_n^z-\delta_n \geq 0, \quad \mbox{if } z = z(n)\\
        \pi_{n,k}-\lambda_n^z+\sigma_n \geq 0, \quad \mbox{if } z \neq z(n)
    \end{cases}
\end{equation*}
Given that an interior point forces $\lambda_n^z=0,\delta_n=0$ and $\sigma_n=0$, and that the prices $\pi_{n,k}$ are positive by definition, both inequalities are always satisfied.

Regarding the Hessian coming from Eq.~\eqref{eq:export_c}, which we call $\nabla^2G_{D_z}$, we can observe that deriving twice we get only constant terms, and given that all the terms in the sum are positive, we obtain a Hessian matrix with only $1$s. Then, since our decision variables are all positive by definition, the requirement $x^{\top}\nabla^2G_{D_z}x\geq0$ is always true.






\section{MADDPG Implementation Details}
\label{app:maddpg}
There are three main problems related to the use of RL algorithms: defining the input state and the output action, mapping the action to satisfy the constraints and deciding the reward function.
\paragraph{State and Action}
We choose the following forms for the input state and output action, for each producer:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{state:} $s^t_z=[p^{t-1}, d^{t-1}, w^{t-1}, \sigma^{t-1}_z]$ where $p^{t-1}$ are the previous day clearing prices, $d^{t-1}$ is the day, $w^{t-1}$ is the day of the week and $\sigma_z^{t-1}$ is a random signal which is zone-specific.
    \item \textbf{action:} $a^t_i=\left[\left[\pi_n^{t,0},\dots,\pi_n^{t,K_n}\right],\left[\Delta_n^{t,0},\dots,\Delta_i^{t,K_n}\right]\right]$, where $K_n$ is the number of bids each producer can submit.
\end{itemize}
\paragraph{Action Mapping}
In our market model we have the following features:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Capacity Output:} we want each capacity bid to be at least 5 MW and less than the maximum capacity, with also the sum being less or equal than the total capacity of the producer.
    \item \textbf{Price Output:} each price bid must be larger than the marginal price $\pi^{min}_n$ and smaller than the maximum bid $\pi^{max}_n$
\end{itemize}
We tackle the two outputs separately, building a module for the capacity and one for the price. We use the following mapping:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Capacity Module:}  The output of the capacity module is a vector of length equal to the length of the bid sequence, which is passed through the soft-max function, representing the percentage of capacity to offer at each step. The benefit of this mapping is that is rather simple, with the drawback of forcing each producer to bid its whole capacity at each bid.
    \item \textbf{Price Module:} The price module produces two values $\alpha,\beta \geq 0$, which are used to obtain the lowest and highest price of the bidding sequence as: $\pi_0 = \min(\pi^{max},\pi^{min}+\min (\alpha,\beta))$ and $\pi_K = \min(\pi^{max}, \pi^{min}+\max(\alpha,\beta))$. We then compute the other prices interpolating between $\pi_0$ and $\pi_K$.
\end{itemize}
\paragraph{Reward Design}
The most direct approach for the reward function in the market environment would be to use the profit, but it does not encode important information: a producer may place a non-optimal bid, but if the demand is high enough, it will be accepted anyway; further the zones have different demands, so a producer in a certain zone may have a smaller profit just because the demand is lower.
What we propose is a reward formed by two terms: a normalized zone profit and a penalty for bid prices that are higher than the clearing price, weighted by the hyperparameter $\beta$:
\begin{equation}
    r^t_n = \sum_{z\in\mathcal{Z}}\left(\frac{p_{n,z}^t}{d^t_z/|\mathcal{N}(z)|} - \beta \sum_{k=1}^{K_n} \mathbb{I}[\lambda_z^t>\pi_n^{min}] \cdot (\pi_n^{t,k}-\lambda_z^t)\right)
\label{eq:reward}
\end{equation}


