An extensive literature exists devoted to color-spanning objects. Basic geometric objects such as the smallest color-spanning circle or square can be computed in $O(nk\log n)$ time~\cite{abellanas2001smallest}. There also exist polynomial-time algorithms to compute other optimal color-spanning objects such as the narrowest strip~\cite{das2009smallest}, the smallest axis-aligned rectangle~\cite{das2009smallest} or square~\cite{khanteimouri2013computing}, the smallest arbitrarily oriented rectangle~\cite{das2009smallest}, the smallest equilateral triangle of fixed orientation~\cite{hasheminejad2015computing}, and the narrowest annulus with the shape of a circle, an axis-parallel square or rectangle, or of an equilateral triangle of fixed orientation~\cite{acharyya2018minimum}. In contrast, the problem of identifying a set of $k$ points of disjoint colors such that the chosen points have the smallest possible diameter is NP-hard~\cite{FleischerX11}.
