Let  ${\cal I}$ be a set of imprecise points modeled as $n$ intervals on the real line $\mathbb{R}$. Each interval is associated with a color with one of $k$ possible colors.  
For a set of colored points, a color-spanning interval contains at least
one point of each color.  
Our problem is to find a point in each interval
such that the length of the smallest color-spanning interval is maximized. We assume the intervals of ${\cal I}$ are given sorted on  $\mathbb{R}$. 



This problem was first introduced in~\cite{acharyya2022minimum}, for a set of disks in 2D  where the authors sketched also a solution for the minimization version; let $R$ be a set of $n$ colored disks where each disk is colored by one of $k$ colors. The problem of finding one point in each disk such that the color-spanning circle of the selected points has the maximum possible radius is NP-hard even for unit input disks~\cite{acharyya2022minimum}. However, the minimization version of the problem can be solved in $O(nk \log n)$ time. For the maximization problem, a $\frac{1}{3}$-factor approximation algorithm (and, $\frac{1}{2}$-factor for pairwise disjoint colors) is also presented in~\cite{acharyya2022minimum}. 

There are a lot of studies in the literature on colored points. Motivated from
data imprecision, the authors of~\cite{ju2013some} showed some hardness results for finding a set of colored points with the largest possible closest pair and some other proximity measures in 2D space.   
The motivation for studying
different variants of color-spanning objects of optimal sizes are described
in~\cite{abellanas2001smallest}. 

%

\paragraph{Contribution.}
The aim of this paper is to explore the 1D  problem studied in~\cite{acharyya2022minimum}. 
We design an $O(n)$ time algorithm for the case where the intervals are pairwise disjoint. We also provide a polynomial-time algorithm for the decision problem for the case where the input is a set of unit intervals of two colors. 

\paragraph{Other related work.}




%Data Imprecision
Several strategies have been proposed in the literature to deal with data imprecision. 
A well-studied formulation is the region-based model~\cite{loffler2009data}.
In this model, the exact location of the input points is uncertain but 
known to fall in a specific region with unspecified distribution. Several problems are studied within this field: finding a placement of points within a set of disks that maximize or minimize the radius of the smallest enclosing circle of the chosen points can be solved in $O(n)$ time~\cite{bbdw}. Finding a placement of points within a set of line segments or squares that maximize or minimize the area or the perimeter of the convex hull can be solved with algorithms of running
times ranging from $O(n)$  to $O(n^{13})$. Some variants are shown to be NP-Hard~\cite{loffler2010largest}. Finding a placement of points within line segments for maximizing or minimizing the area of the convex $k$-gons can be solved with algorithms of running
times ranging from $O(n^2)$  to $O(n^{10})$~\cite{Keikhat}. Finding a placement of points within a set of squares or disks for maximizing or minimizing the area of the bounding box can be solved in running
times ranging from $O(n)$ to $O(n \log n)$. 
Other problems that have been studied in the region-based model are computing a placement of points to maximize or minimize the diameter~\cite{bbdw,Keikhaad} or width~\cite{keikha2018width} 
on a set of line segments, squares, or disks. 
Computing the minimum weight spanning tree over all possible trees of the points within the disk regions is also studied and shown to be NP-hard~\cite{dorrigiv2015minimum}. 
Another optimization problem is computing $k$ points on a real line 
to minimize the maximum of minimum or maximum possible distances of uncertain points in the form of segments coincident with the input line to their closest centers, which can be solved in $O(n)$ time~\cite{hu2022computing}. 
Also, for a set of $n$ weighted intervals on a line, the problem of finding $k$ 
 points on the line to partition it into $k+1$
 segments, such that the sum of the weights of the intervals is minimized can be solved in $O(n \log n)$ time~\cite{cao2015balanced}. %See also~\cite{li2018geometric}. 
A non-optimization objective in the imprecise setup is computing a data structure to efficiently compute the Delaunay triangulation of any possible placement of the points within the disk regions~\cite{loffler2008delaunay}.  
