\begin{theorem}
The \kda~ problem can be solved in $O(n)$ time.  
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
To proof the correctness of the algorithm, (i) observe that the assignment a particular d-\mcsi~ locally maximizes the length of the minimum color-spanning interval. Now, as this assignment is independent of any other assignment, thus assigning points to those d-\mcsi~ do not change any other independent d-\mcsi. Thus, we obtain a global assignment. (ii) For each d-\mcsi~we have obtained the maximum length \mcsi. Next, considering all the solutions we return the minimum one as our desired $\lmcsi$. Thus, the $\lmcsi$ is the smallest color-spanning interval for the returned realization, which is also the maximum one for any possible realization. Thus, our algorithm returns the correct solution. 

Let there are $m$ d-\mcsi~ and each d-\mcsi~contains $i$ many intervals, for $i=\{1,2,\ldots,n\}$, where $\displaystyle\sum _{i=1}^m i =n$. Now, each d-\mcsi~takes $i$ amount of time (see \cite{li2018dispersing}) to find the realization corresponding to the maximum length minimum color-spanning interval. Thus, overall finding the realization to all the intervals corresponding to the $\lmcsi$ takes $O(n)$ time. \qed 
\end{proof}



\subsection{Decision problem for $\frac 1 2 < q\leq \frac 3 4$}


\paragraph{Notation used} (will have to be changed to make it consistent with the rest). $I_i$ for the i$th$ interval and $x_i$ for its left endpoint (assuming intervals are numbered from left to right).

\begin{definition}
A \emph{leftmost solution} is a solution where each point, starting from the leftmost, and proceeding from left to right, is as much to the left as possible with respect to the other points to its left. 
\end{definition}

\begin{observation}
In a leftmost solution, each point is either at the left endpoint of its interval, or at a distance of multiple of $q$ from some other left endpoint, located to its left.
\end{observation} 

\begin{observation} If there is a solution, there is one that is leftmost.
\end{observation}


\begin{definition}
A \emph{tabular (sub)solution} for $I_i$--$I_j$ is a leftmost (sub)solution, that starts at $I_i$ and ends at $I_j$, where consecutive points (at different $x$-coordinates) are at a distance of $q$, and, in addition, it is \emph{complete}: it contains points for, at least, all intervals between $i$ and $j$.
%if there is a point of a certain color placed a coordinate $x_0$, then all intervals of the same color---between $i$ and $j$---that contain $x_0$ also have their point placed at $x_0$.\footnote{This only applies to the $x$-coordinates that correspond to points at positions ``multiples of $q$''. The current phrasing probably can be made more clear.} 
\end{definition}

\maria{We should probably point out that in a tabular (sub)solution colors alternate.}

The following follows from the definition of leftmost solution.

\begin{lemma}  Any leftmost solution is a sequence of tabular subsolutions.
\end{lemma} 
\begin{proof}

\end{proof}

The sequence of tabular subsolutions can be obtained by going through the solution from left to right: a new tabular subsolution starts each time a point is placed at an endpoint.

