\begin{definition} A \emph{maximal tabular solution} is a tabular solution that cannot be extended further to the right. 
\end{definition}
%\begin{obs}  A solution is  maximal tabular if and only if  no interval starts at distance less than $q$ from its rightmost point, and with opposite color.
%\end{obs} 

Some of the definitions above are illustrated in Figures~\ref{fig:tabular} and~\ref{fig:tabular2}.
Note that $T_1$ only contains one point because it cannot be extended further: doing so requires placing the point of $I_2$ at distance $q$ from the point of $I_1$, but that would leave no feasible point for $I_3$.

We can observe the following.

\begin{observation}
A maximal tabular subsolution ends because:
(i) there is no other interval of color opposite to that of its rightmost point that begins within distance $q$;\maria{I am not sure ``begins within distance $q$" is what we need; don't we want some interval of the right color that passes through the next point at distance $q$, even if this interval had already started when we placed the previous point?}
(ii) placing the next point at distance last $+q$ would create an infeasible solution with another interval further to the right.\maria{This is not consistent with the algorithm $M[i]$ below, where we check if placing the next point at distance last $+q$ would create an infeasible solution with another interval, not matter if left or right.}
\end{observation}

We will use $M[i]$ to denote a maximal tabular subsolution that starts at $I_i$.


For example, in Figure~\ref{fig:tabular} the  subsolution $M[1]$ is $T_1$, and the subsolution $M[3]$ is $T_2$.

Note that  $M[i]$ can include points of intervals (of the opposite color) that begin to the left of $i$, because the point at $x_i$ forces those to be placed at $x_i+q$.


For the subsolution $M[i]$ we will store, in addition to $x_i$, the position of its rightmost point(s) placed. Note that since since we know the color of $I_i$, and we know that all the positions of points placed in $M[i]$ are at distance $q$ from each other, this is enough to deduce the colors and positions of all points in $M[i]$. 

\subsubsection{Computing $M[i]$}

Assume w.l.o.g. that $I_i$ is red, and recall that $x_i$ is its left endpoint.
We begin by placing the point of $I_i$ at $x_i$, and proceed in the following way with $x_r:=x_i$ and $M[i]:=x_i$ (recall we store the rightmost point placed).

\begin{enumerate}
\item If there are blue intervals not compatible with a red point at $x_r$ (i.e., blue intervals with left endpoint in $(x_r-q, x_r-(1-q) $), revert last placement of red point(s), updating $M[i$] accordingly, and stop.

\item If there are red intervals starting to the right of $x_r$ that are not compatible with a blue point at $x_r+q$ (i.e., red intervals with left endpoint in $(x_r,x_r+2q-1)$), stop.

\item For all blue intervals starting in $[x_r-(1-q), x_r+q]$, set their points at $x_r+q$ (and update $M[i]$). If there are no blue intervals in this situation, stop.

\item For all red intervals starting in $[x_r+2q-1, x_r+2q]$, set their point at $x_r+2q$ (and update $M[i]$).

\item Repeat steps 1--4, taking $x_r := x_r+2q$.
\end{enumerate}

\begin{lemma}
\label{lem:tabular_comp}
If all intervals are given in sorted order from left to right, $M[i]$ can be computed in time linear in its size.
\end{lemma}


\subsubsection{Computing (sub)solutions}

