Note that $L[k]$ may contain points placed further to the right of $x_i$, but in that case, when combining $L[k]$ and $M[i]$, only the points up to $x_i$ will be used. However, some of them may have to be relocated, as specified next.

\begin{lemma}
If $L[k]$ is compatible with $M[i]$ then $L[k]$ and $M[i]$ can be combined by taking the assignment of $L[k]$ up to $i-1$, except for 
(i) the blue points of intervals starting in $(x_i-(1-q),x_i)$, which are placed at $x_i+q$; and
(ii) the red points of intervals containing $x_i$, which are placed at $x_i$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Recall that $x_i$ is assumed, without loss of generality, to be red.
Consider the solution $L[k]$ up to $x_i$, that is, all points placed at coordinate up to $x_i$.
All these correspond to intervals that begin to the left of $x_i$.
Since $M[i]$ and $L[k]$ are compatible, all the  blue points of $L[k]$ placed after $x_i-q$ can be relocated at $x_i+q$.
It remains to argue that placing one or more blue points at $x_i+q$ does not create conflicts with $M[i]$.
That can be the case only if there are red intervals starting at $(x_r,x_r+2q-1)$, but in this case, we would have $M[i]=\{ x_i \}$, so we do not need to consider conflicts further to the right than $x_i$.
\rodrigo{here it matters a bit how we define $M[i]$ exactly}
\end{proof}

One can check compatibility efficiently.

\begin{lemma}
\label{lem:comp_check}
The intervals can be preprocesed in $O(n \log n)$ time so that, given $L[k]$ and $M[i]$, one can check if they are compatible in $O(1)$ time.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
It follows from Def.~\ref{def:compatible} that we need to determine if all points in $L[k]$ with color opposite to that of $I_i$ that are located in $[x_i-q, x_i]$ can be relocated at $x_i+q$.
This would be straightforward if we would store the full solution of $L[k]$, but since we only store $O(1)$ information with $L[k]$, it requires some extra work.

In a preprocessing phase, we check for each $i$ if there is an interval of opposite color starting  between $x_i-q$ and $x_i-(1-q)$;
if that is the case, no solution can contain a point of the color of $I_i$ at $x_i$, and we can already set $M[i]=L[i]=\emptyset$.\rodrigo{If the definition of $M[i]$ is already $\emptyset$ for this case, then this can be omitted}
This can be done easily after sorting the red and blue interval endpoints, separately, in $O(n \log n)$ time.

In the remaining, assume w.l.o.g. that $I_i$ is red. If $M[i]\neq \emptyset$, $L[k]$ can still contain blue points in $[x_i-q,x_i]$ with intervals starting before  $x_i-q$ or  after $x_i-(1-q)$.
All points with intervals starting after $x_i-(1-q)$ can be relocated at $x_i+q$, so they can be ignored.
It follows that $L[k]$ and $M[i]$ are compatible if and only if there is no blue point in $[x_i-q,x_i]$ with interval starting before  $x_i-q$. 

Suppose such a blue point exists. Let $b_l$ be its $x$-coordinate (note that if there is more than one, they must have the same coordinate).
Observe that $b_l$ cannot be a left interval endpoint, since its interval begins before $x_i-q$ and $b_l \in [x_i-q, x_i]$.

If $b_l > x_k$, then $b_l$ is a position of the form $x_k+m \cdot q$, for some integer $m>0$. 
In particular, it is the rightmost blue point in $M[k]$ to the left of $x_i$. 
Therefore, we can compute it in $O(1)$ time (assuming the floor operation takes constant time).

If $b_l \leq x_k$, $b_l$ is at distance less than $q$ from $x_k$ (since $x_k < x_i$).
Then observe that $I_k$ and $b_l$ must have the same color; otherwise, $b_l$ would not be compatible with $x_k$.
Then $b_l$ must be equal to a piece of information that is stored with $L[k]$: the rightmost blue point of $L[k]$ with $x$-coordinate smaller than $x_k$ that is \emph{not} placed at a left endpoint.\rodrigo{We may want to give a shorter name to this `piece of information' that we store}
Therefore we know $b_l$, and we can check if  $b_l \in [x_i-q, x_i]$ in $O(1)$ time.
\end{proof}

We have now a simple algorithm to compute a solution to the original problem, if one exists. 
%Since we are interested in finding a solution from 1 to $n$, we will only consider subsolutions that have points starting from $I_1$.


\begin{enumerate}

    \item Compute each maximal tabular solution $M[i]$, one from each $i$, from $i=1$ to $n$.
    By Lemma~\ref{lem:tabular_comp}, this can be done in total $O(n^2)$ time.

    \item For each $i$, from 2 to $n$, check if $M[i]$ is compatible with some previous solution $L[k]$.
    If there exists some $k$ such that $L[k]$ and $M[i]$ are compatible, set $L[i]$ ``equal'' to their combination.
    Otherwise, $L[i] = \emptyset$.
    Note that when setting $L[i]$, we only store $O(1)$ information. In particular, we store (i) the position of the rightmost placed point of $M[i]$ (this identifies the rightmost maximal tabular subsolution of $L[i]$), and 
    (ii) the position of the rightmost point to the left of $x_i$ that is of the same color as $I_i$.
    By Lemma~\ref{lem:comp_check}, this step takes $O(n^2)$ time in total.
\end{enumerate}

The original problem will have a solution if there exists an $i$ such that $L[i]$ that contains points from 1 to $n$. 


\begin{theorem}
A solution when $\frac 1 2 < q\leq \frac 3 4$ can be found in $O(n^2)$ time, assuming the floor operation takes constant time.
\end{theorem}




