\subsection{Reactive Planning}
\label{ssec:planning}
In our reactive \emph{planning} algorithm the outcome is a command in longitudinal velocity and yaw rate that is forwarded to the low-level hierarchical PID originally implemented in the Crazyflie firmware. Overall, we compute a repulsive action that keeps the drone away from obstacles in a semi-unknown, or partially known environment. The known components are the dimensions of the area to explore and the shape of the obstacles (not their location). From the area dimensions, a plan is retrieved in terms of waypoints to traverse. We limited our study to the traversal of the space between two waypoints; the method can then be replicated for more target locations.

We use 2D coordinates in the path planning for simplicity. The drone starts from a waypoint $P_A$ to reach a target $P_B$. We denote the estimated pose as $\hat{P}$. 


