\noindent\textbf{Cross-Domain Pre-Training.}
Table~\ref{tab:cross-domain pre-train} presents results on cross-domain pre-training using three diverse graph types: Arxiv (academic network), FB15K237 (knowledge graph), and HIV (molecular graph) \citep{liu2024one}. All graphs are text-attributed; we use a shared textual encoder to project node descriptions into a unified embedding space, enabling a single model to operate across domains. We compare \ours to pre-training baselines (BGRL \citep{thakoor2022largescale}, GraphMAE \citep{hou2022graphmae}) and graph foundation models (OFA \citep{liu2024one}, GFT \citep{wang2024gft}). \ours achieves the best or second-best performance across all datasets, highlighting its superior ability to learn transferable substructure representations, even under significant domain shifts—where message-passing-based methods often struggle.

\section{Related Works}

% \noindent\textbf{Graph Transformers.} Inspired by the success of Transformer architectures on vision and language \citep{devlin2019bert,brown2020language,touvron2023llama,he2022masked,dosovitskiy2021an}, researchers adopt such architecture on graphs \citep{kreuzer2021rethinking, dwivedi2020generalization, rampasek2022recipe,he2023generalization,chen2023nagphormer,chen2022structure}. However, comparing to the dominant of message-passing, graph transformer is at a non-mainstream position. Basically, graph transformers tokenize graphs into node sequences, which are fed into a Transformer for encoding node embeddings \citep{kreuzer2021rethinking, dwivedi2020generalization, rampasek2022recipe}. Generally, positional embeddings \citep{you2019position,dwivedi2022graph} will be added on each node to inject structural knowledge. However, the quadratic complexity of all-pair node attention poses significant computational challenges, making such methods are more suitable for small graphs like molecules but impractical on large-scale graphs with more than millions of nodes \citep{wu2023simplifying}. To overcome the limitation, researchers use graph substructures \citep{yanardag2015deep,ivanov2018anonymous} to form sequences that describe graph instances \citep{wang2025neural,he2023generalization,chen2023nagphormer}. An example is GPM \citep{wang2025neural}, which takes random walks to sample substructure sequences and use Transformer to aggregate the substructures, doing an ViT-style learning on graphs. Based on such architecture, we investigate the potential of doing Transformer generative pre-training on graphs.

% \noindent\textbf{Self-Supervised Pre-Training.} Generative pre-training, no matter next-token prediction \citep{brown2020language,tian2024visual} or masked-token prediction \citep{devlin2019bert,xie2022simmim,bao2021beit,he2022masked}, have dominant vision and language pre-training. Basically, such methdos use visible tokens to predict invisible tokens, which has been proved to be effective in modeling generalizable knowledge from large volume of data, making the success of modern foundation models \citep{touvron2023llama}. This Transformer generative pre-training paradigm have reshaping many domains, including video, audio, biology, embodied AI, etc, but not graphs.

% On graphs, the pre-training strategies is dominanted by the contrative methods \citep{you2020graph,zhu2021graph,hassani2020contrastive,qiu2020gcc}, which creates two corrupted views to predict each to model the augmentation-invariance on graphs. However, such contrastive learning approaches have been proved to be less effective than generative learning strategies on vision and language. Someone also investigate the generative pre-training via masked graph prediction (VGAE, GraphMAE, GraphMAE2, MaskGAE, ...). However, these methods design the reconstruction low-semantic objective like nodes or links, rather than high-level semantics like substructures. In addition, these methods are based on message-passing as the backbone, showing limited expressiveness, less scalability, etc. In contrast to them, \ours is a message-passing-free Transformer framework, achieving better scalability.

% \noindent\textbf{Random Walks on Graphs.} Using random walks to represent substructures on graphs have been widely applied, from primitive training paradigms \citep{perozzi2014deepwalk,grover2016node2vec,yanardag2015deep,ivanov2018anonymous} to state-of-the-art deep learning algorithms \citep{wang2025neural,zhao2022from,jin2022raw, martinkus2023agentbased, tonshoff2023walking}. For example, \citet{wang2025neural} splits random walks into semantic walks and anonymous walks to jointly model the semantic and structural knowledge. In addition, numerous works have proved these random walk methods potentially breaking the constaints of message-passing, like improving expressiveness \citep{welke2023expectation,michel2023path}, modeling long-range dependencies \citep{martinkus2023agentbased, tonshoff2023walking}, mitigating over-smoothing and over-squashing \citep{wang2024nonconvolutional}. Despite their strengths, these methods tend to emphasize long-range dependencies at the expense of localized information \citep{tonshoff2023walking} and lack interpretability regarding the specific graph knowledge being learned.

\noindent\textbf{Graph Transformers.}
Inspired by the success of Transformers in vision and language \citep{devlin2019bert,brown2020language,touvron2023llama,he2022masked,dosovitskiy2021an}, several works have extended this architecture to graphs \citep{kreuzer2021rethinking,dwivedi2020generalization,rampasek2022recipe,he2023generalization,chen2023nagphormer,chen2022structure, zhang2024diet, zhang2024mopi}. Typically, graph Transformers treat nodes as tokens and apply self-attention over all pairs. However, the quadratic complexity of node attention limits their scalability, making them impractical for large graphs \citep{wu2023simplifying}. To address this, recent works propose tokenizing graphs via substructures such as random walks or motifs \citep{yanardag2015deep,ivanov2018anonymous}, enabling sequence-based modeling \citep{wang2025neural,chen2023nagphormer,he2023generalization}. For example, GPM \citep{wang2025neural} applies a ViT-style architecture over substructure sequences. Building on this idea, our work explores generative pretraining using a Transformer over substructures, entirely removing message passing.

% \noindent\textbf{Self-Supervised Pretraining.}
\noindent\textbf{Generative Pretraining.}
Generative pretraining has driven major advances in vision and language. These methods predict masked content from visible context, enabling learning from large-scale unlabeled data and powering modern foundation models \citep{touvron2023llama}. This paradigm has extended to other domains (e.g., video, audio, biology), but its impact on graphs remains limited.
% Graph pretraining has been dominated by contrastive methods \citep{you2020graph,zhu2021graph,hassani2020contrastive,qiu2020gcc}, which learn invariant representations via view alignment. While effective, contrastive learning often underperforms compared to generative objectives in other domains. 
More recent works apply generative approaches to graphs (e.g., VGAE \citep{kipf2016variational}, GraphMAE \citep{hou2022graphmae}, GraphMAE2 \citep{hou2023graphmae2}, MaskGAE \citep{li2023s}), but focus on reconstructing low-level signals such as nodes or links, and typically rely on message-passing GNNs, limiting their expressiveness and scalability. In contrast, \ours introduces a fully Transformer-based, message-passing-free framework that models high-level semantic substructures, unlocking better scalability and generalization.

\noindent\textbf{Random Walks on Graphs.}
Random walks have been widely used to represent substructures, from early unsupervised embeddings \citep{perozzi2014deepwalk,grover2016node2vec,yanardag2015deep,ivanov2018anonymous} to recent deep models \citep{wang2025neural,zhao2022from,jin2022raw,martinkus2023agentbased,tonshoff2023walking}.
% For example, \citet{wang2025neural} separates semantic and anonymous walks to jointly capture feature and structural patterns. 
These methods have been shown to break key limitations of message passing, improving expressiveness \citep{welke2023expectation,michel2023path}, modeling long-range dependencies \citep{martinkus2023agentbased,tonshoff2023walking}, and mitigating over-smoothing and over-squashing \citep{wang2024nonconvolutional}. However, these random walk-based methods often focus on global patterns while overlooking localized structures \citep{tonshoff2023walking}. \ours uses random walks to sample substructures and design the MSM task to automatically balance the contributions between localized and globalized substructures.

\section{Conclusion}

% In this work, we design \oursws, a Transformer-based generative graph pre-training method going beyond message-passing. Compared to message-passing methods, \ours has much more better scalability, i.e., adding more data and increasing the number of parameters bring better performance, yet message-passing quickly encounters saturate points. We perform comprehensive ablation to bring valuable insights on model design, and do extensive experiments to show the effectiveness and transferability of \oursws.

% However, the sampled substructure sequences are un-ordered in \oursws, which enables the masked-token prediction but not next-token prediction, which is a more scalable approach on images \citep{tian2024visual} and text \citep{brown2020language}. Thus, designing ordered substructure sequences is a promising direction. In addition, due to the great scalability and applicability of \oursws, it is worth to further explore the potential of \ours in serving as the backbone of graph foundation models. Lastly, \ours applies random walk to sample substructure patterns, which is an online tokenization strategy rather than a fixed tokenizer. Designing a learnable graph substructure tokenization and efficient leverage method is another direction.


We introduce \oursws, a Transformer-based generative pretraining framework for graphs that operates entirely without message passing. Unlike traditional GNNs, \ours scales effectively with both data and model size, and consistently improves performance across tasks. Through extensive ablations and experiments, we demonstrate its expressiveness, transferability, and potential as a scalable backbone for graph representation learning. \textbf{Limitation and Future Works:} While \ours leverages unordered substructure sequences---suitable for masked-token prediction---extending it to ordered sequences may enable next-token prediction, further improving scalability. Additionally, our use of random walks as an online tokenizer opens up future directions in designing learnable and adaptive substructure tokenizers for graphs.

